OAI_PAPER({"slug": "The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026", "title": "The Restricted Geometric Langlands Equivalence in Positive Characteristic", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/The-Restricted-Geometric-Langlands-Equivalence-in-Positive-Characteristic-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "014", "family_title": "Restricted geometric Langlands, global Arthur enhancements, and generic Ramanujan", "abstract_html": "We prove the \\(\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\)-linear restricted geometric Langlands equivalence for smooth projective connected curves and connected reductive groups in characteristic <i>p</i> &amp;gt; 0, with <i>ℓ</i> ≠ <i>p</i>, in two regimes. Over \\(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q\\), we assume the four characteristic conditions of the restricted theory stated below. Over arbitrary algebraically closed fields, we assume these conditions and additionally that <i>p</i> is very good and does not divide the Weyl-group order. This proves Gaitsgory–Raskin's full-support conjecture [[12, Conjecture 1.3.10]](https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.02237v1) in these regimes.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "Theorem 1.4 (Full support)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k\\) be an algebraically closed field of characteristic \\(p&gt;0\\), let \\(\\ell\\ne p\\), and put \\(E=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_{\\ell}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X/k\\) be a smooth projective connected curve and \\(G/k\\) a connected reductive group. Write \\(\\check G/E\\) for the Langlands dual group and \\(\\mathfrak g=\\operatorname{Lie}(G)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Impose **Hypothesis 1.1**:</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">1. There is a nondegenerate \\(G\\)-invariant symmetric bilinear form \\(\\kappa\\) on \\(\\mathfrak g\\), whose restriction to the center of \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(M)\\) is nondegenerate for every Levi subgroup \\(M\\).</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">2. For every Levi subgroup \\(M\\), including \\(G\\), and maximal torus \\(T_M\\subset M\\), Chevalley restriction is an isomorphism\n     \\[\n     k[\\operatorname{Lie}(M)]^M\n     \\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n     k[\\operatorname{Lie}(T_M)]^{W_M},\n     \\qquad W_M=N_M(T_M)/T_M.\n     \\]</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">3. The scheme-theoretic centralizer in \\(G\\) of every semisimple element of \\(\\mathfrak g\\) is a Levi subgroup.</p><p class=\"li l2 num\">4. For every extension \\(k'/k\\), every nilpotent element of \\(\\mathfrak g\\otimes_k k'\\) belongs to \\(\\operatorname{Lie}(R_u(P))\\) for a parabolic subgroup \\(P\\subset G_{k'}\\) defined over \\(k'\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume one of the following regimes:</p><p class=\"li l2\">**Hypothesis 1.2, regime A:** \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q\\), and \\(X\\) and \\(G\\) are obtained from a smooth projective geometrically connected curve \\(X_0/\\mathbb F_q\\) and a split connected reductive group \\(G_0/\\mathbb F_q\\). No Weyl-group-order restriction is added.</p><p class=\"li l2\">**Hypothesis 1.3, regime B:** \\(k\\) is arbitrary algebraically closed, \\(p\\) is very good for \\(G\\), and \\(p\\nmid |W_G|\\). No finite-field descent of \\(X\\) is assumed.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the ind-constructible sheaf categories of [3,12]. The category \\(\\operatorname{QLisse}_E(X)\\) consists of objects whose ordinary cohomology sheaves are filtered colimits of finite-rank lisse \\(E\\)-sheaves.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the restricted spectral prestack by\n  \\[\n  Y=\\operatorname{LS}^{\\mathrm{restr}}_{\\check G}(X),\\qquad\n  Y(T)=\n  \\operatorname{Fun}^{\\otimes,\\mathrm{r.t.ex}}\n  \\bigl(\\operatorname{Rep}_E(\\check G),\n  \\operatorname{QCoh}(T)\\otimes_E\\operatorname{QLisse}_E(X)\\bigr)\n  \\]\n  for derived affine \\(E\\)-schemes \\(T\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G(X))\\) be the automorphic category defined by the global nilpotent cone, and let \\(\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y)\\) use the spectral nilpotent singular-support condition.</p><p class=\"li\">The established restricted Langlands functor factors as\n  \\[\n  \\mathbb L_G^{\\mathrm{restr}}:\n  \\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G(X))\n  \\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n  \\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y')\n  \\hookrightarrow\n  \\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y),\n  \\]\n  where \\(Y'\\subset Y\\) is an open-and-closed union of connected components, and the functor is \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\)-linear [12, Lemma 1.3.7 and Main Theorem 1.3.9].</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The inclusion \\(Y'\\subset Y\\) is equality as spectral prestacks. Consequently the given functor is a \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\)-linear equivalence\n\\[\n\\mathbb L_G^{\\mathrm{restr}}:\n\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G(X))\n\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y).\n\\]\nThis proves [12, Conjecture 1.3.10] in the two stated regimes.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose \\(Y'\\ne Y\\). Since \\(Y'\\) is a union of open-and-closed components, choose \\(C\\subset Y\\) disjoint from \\(Y'\\). Proposition 1 supplies a nonzero compact \\(F\\) with all the stated specialization and Hecke vanishings.</p><p>There is a \\(\\overline K\\)-valued bundle where its stalk is nonzero: on a finite-type smooth chart, a nonzero constructible complex has a nonzero stalk at a closed point. Proposition 7 extends that bundle after an allowed finite trait replacement. The geometric generic field remains \\(\\overline K\\), and the vanishings remain valid.</p><p>Choose a finite-type smooth affine chart \\(b:U\\to\\mathcal B\\) through the extended bundle’s special value. Its fiber product with the trait section is smooth over \\(S\\) and has a \\(k\\)-point. It has an \\(R\\)-point lifting it: after an affine étale neighborhood, formal smoothness gives compatible lifts modulo all powers of the uniformizer, and completeness gives the point. Thus the trait section lifts to \\(U\\).</p><p>Shrink around its closed point so that \\(U/S\\) has constant relative dimension \\(N\\). The generic stalk of \\(F_U=b_{\\overline K}^*F\\) on that section is nonzero, so the closure of its nonzero-stalk locus meets \\(U_s\\).</p><p>Let \\(\\mathcal N_U\\subset T^*U_s\\) be the smooth-chart pullback of the global nilpotent cone. Under either regime, the nilpotent-parabolic assumption and invariant form give the half-dimensionality theorem [3, Appendix D.1.1–D.1.8], whose smooth-chart formulation is\n\\[\n\\dim\\mathcal N_U\\le\\dim U_s=N\n\\]\n[3, Appendix D.1.2].</p><p>Apply Proposition 8 to \\(\\mathcal F=F_U\\) and \\(\\Lambda=\\mathcal N_U\\). It yields a slice \\(J=V(f_1,\\ldots,f_d)\\) and \\(u\\in J_s(k)\\) such that\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_J(F_U|_{J_{\\overline K}})\\bigr)_u\\ne0,\n\\]\nwhile its independent differentials span a plane meeting \\((\\mathcal N_U)_u\\) only at zero.</p><p>Proposition 6 applies to the same chart and slice and makes that stalk zero. This contradiction includes \\(d=0\\), when the test is the original smooth-chart vanishing.</p><p>Therefore the complementary union is empty, \\(Y'=Y\\), and the established primed equivalence is the asserted full restricted equivalence.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 2", "orig": "Theorem 1.5 (Rational spectral support)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(E_0=\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\) and \\(E=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q\\), let \\(X/k\\) be smooth projective connected, and let \\(G/k\\) be connected reductive satisfying Hypothesis 1.1 stated in Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\check G_0/E_0\\) be the split dual group and \\(\\check G=\\check G_0\\times_{E_0}E\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define \\(Y_0\\) by the tensor-functor formula of Theorem 1 with \\(E_0\\) in place of \\(E\\), and put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_0=\n  \\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp},E_0}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G).\n  \\]\n  Coefficient extension does not change the geometric field \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The rational spectral-action datum is given: a continuous \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y_0)\\)-action on \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) whose restriction along universal evaluation is the usual multi-leg Hecke system, with tensor, permutation, and collision compatibilities.</p><p class=\"li\">No normalized rational Langlands equivalence is assumed to have been constructed.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every nonempty open-and-closed substack \\(U\\subset Y_0\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\operatorname{QCoh}(U)\n\\underset{\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y_0)}{\\otimes}\n\\mathcal C_0\\ne 0.\n\\]\nConsequently, if \\(Y'_0\\subset Y_0\\) is an open-and-closed support union whose complementary structure idempotent acts by zero on \\(\\mathcal C_0\\), then \\(Y'_0=Y_0\\) as spectral prestacks. This applies in particular to the support of any given \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y_0)\\)-linear primed equivalence.</p><p>The assertion concerns the whole rational spectral prestack, without identifying its components with rational points. No rational extension of regime B is asserted.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(X\\ne\\mathbb P^1\\), Propositions 10 and 11 compare sheaf categories, spectral prestacks, and universal evaluation; Proposition 12 identifies the spectral actions.</p><p>The curve and split group descend to a finite field by finite presentation. Theorem 1 in regime A supplies the full \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\)-linear equivalence required in Proposition 13, without an additional Weyl-order restriction.</p><p>If a nonempty open-and-closed localization were zero, its structure idempotent would act by zero. Proposition 13 would make that substack empty, a contradiction. Thus every nonempty clopen localization is nonzero.</p><p>For \\(X=\\mathbb P^1\\), the geometric restricted spectral prestack \\(Y\\) has one connected component. Components are indexed by semisimple geometric local systems [3, Proposition 3.7.2], and the geometric étale fundamental group of \\(\\mathbb P^1\\) is trivial. Indeed, for a connected finite étale cover \\(C\\to\\mathbb P^1\\) of degree \\(n\\), Riemann–Hurwitz gives\n\\[\n2g(C)-2=-2n,\n\\]\nso \\(n=1\\) [24, Tag 0C1B].</p><p>Proposition 11 and faithful-flat affine testing, as in Proposition 13, imply that \\(Y_0\\) has no nonempty proper clopen substack. The category \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) is nonzero: its dualizing object has shifted constant restrictions on smooth charts and zero singular support. Hence its only nonempty clopen localization, the whole category, is nonzero.</p><p>Finally, the stipulated complement of \\(Y'_0\\) acts by zero and is therefore empty. A given spectral-linear primed equivalence has this property by linearity, so its actual support inclusion is equality.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 3", "orig": "Corollary 8.1 (The category on a connected component)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume either regime A or regime B of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_s=\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G),\n  \\qquad\n  A=\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal D=\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For an open-and-closed connected component \\(i:Z\\hookrightarrow Y\\), define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_{s,Z}\n  =\n  \\operatorname{QCoh}(Z)\\underset{A}{\\otimes}\\mathcal C_s.\n  \\]\n  The structure idempotent \\(e_Z=i_*\\mathcal O_Z\\) acts as the projection to this summand.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every connected component \\(Z\\subset Y\\) has a nonzero localized automorphic category. Restriction of the given spectral-linear Langlands functor induces an equivalence\n\\[\n\\mathcal C_{s,Z}\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Z).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The decomposition\n\\[\nY=Z\\sqcup(Y\\setminus Z)\n\\]\ndecomposes quasi-coherent and nilpotent ind-coherent sheaves into the corresponding binary summands. Hence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{QCoh}(Z)\\underset{A}{\\otimes}\\mathcal D\n\\simeq\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Z).\n\\]\nTensor the \\(A\\)-linear equivalence of Theorem 1 with \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Z)\\) over \\(A\\). This gives the equivalence by the specified Langlands functor.</p><p>Every component contains a semisimple \\(E\\)-point [3, Proposition 3.7.2 and Corollary 3.7.4]. Pullback there shows \\(\\mathcal O_Z\\ne0\\), and the fully faithful embedding\n\\[\n\\operatorname{QCoh}(Z)\\hookrightarrow\n\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Z)\n\\]\nproves nonvanishing.</p><p>Two geometric parameters lie in the same component precisely when their semisimplifications are isomorphic; this component indexing does not discard the actual parameter’s extension data.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 4", "orig": "Corollary 8.2 (The arithmetic formula)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume regime A of Theorem 1, with models \\(X_0,G_0\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\operatorname{Frob}\\) be the automorphism of \\(Y\\) induced by pullback under geometric \\(q\\)-Frobenius.</p><p class=\"li\">Define the derived fixed-point stack\n  \\[\n  Y^{\\mathrm{arithm}}\n  :=\n  Y^{\\operatorname{Frob}}\n  =\n  Y\\underset{Y\\times_EY}{\\times^{\\mathrm R}}Y,\n  \\]\n  using the diagonal and graph of \\(\\operatorname{Frob}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">An \\(E\\)-point of \\(Y^{\\mathrm{arithm}}\\) includes a Weil structure on its geometric local system.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\Gamma^{\\operatorname{IndCoh}}\\) for ind-coherent global sections and \\(\\omega\\) for the ind-coherent dualizing object.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There is a canonical isomorphism\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Funct}_c(\\operatorname{Bun}_{G_0}(\\mathbb F_q),E)\n\\simeq\n\\Gamma^{\\operatorname{IndCoh}}\n\\bigl(Y^{\\mathrm{arithm}},\\omega_{Y^{\\mathrm{arithm}}}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe left side is compactly supported functions on isomorphism classes of rational bundles, regarded as a complex concentrated in degree zero. The right side uses the derived fixed-point stack, not its classical truncation, and its dualizing object, not its structure sheaf.</p>", "proof": "<p>The primed union \\(Y'\\) is Frobenius-stable. The spectral trace calculation of Beraldo–Lin–Reeves [6, §§6.4.12–6.4.13] enters the known primed formula [12, §§1.5.3–1.5.5 and Corollary 1.5.6]:\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Funct}_c(\\operatorname{Bun}_{G_0}(\\mathbb F_q),E)\n\\simeq\n\\Gamma^{\\operatorname{IndCoh}}\n\\bigl((Y')^{\\operatorname{Frob}},\n\\omega_{(Y')^{\\operatorname{Frob}}}\\bigr).\n\\]\nThe automorphic trace input is [2, Theorem 0.2.6], with endofunctor pushforward under geometric Frobenius on \\(\\operatorname{Bun}_G\\).</p><p>Theorem 1 identifies the actual inclusion \\(Y'\\hookrightarrow Y\\) with equality. Derived fixed points therefore identify \\((Y')^{\\operatorname{Frob}}\\) with \\(Y^{\\mathrm{arithm}}\\), carrying the same dualizing object and ind-coherent global-sections functor. Substitute this equality in the known primed formula.</p><p>Keeping \\(\\Gamma^{\\operatorname{IndCoh}}\\) explicit preserves the convention explained in [12, §1.5.3], rather than replacing the derived fixed-point stack by its classical truncation or its dualizing object by its structure sheaf.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 5", "orig": "Corollary 8.5 (Hecke eigenobjects with coherent structure), using Definition 8.4", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume either regime A or regime B of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\sigma:\\operatorname{Spec}E\\to Y\\) be any parameter.</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite set \\(I\\), put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal Q_I=\\operatorname{QLisse}(X)^{\\otimes I}.\n  \\]\n  For \\(V\\in\\operatorname{Rep}(\\check G)^{\\otimes I}\\), let\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal E_V^I\\in\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\otimes\\mathcal Q_I\n  \\]\n  be universal evaluation and set\n  \\[\n  \\sigma^I(V)=(\\sigma^*\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(\\mathcal E_V^I).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathrm H_I(V,-)\\) be the multi-leg Hecke functor.</p><p class=\"li\">A coherent Hecke eigenstructure of eigenvalue \\(\\sigma\\) on \\(M\\) consists of isomorphisms\n  \\[\n  \\mathrm H_I(V,M)\\simeq M\\boxtimes\\sigma^I(V)\n  \\]\n  for all finite \\(I\\), natural in \\(V\\), and homotopy-coherently compatible with units, tensor products, permutations, and collisions of legs.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every parameter \\(\\sigma\\) admits a nonzero\n\\[\nM_\\sigma\\in\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G)\n\\]\nwith a coherent Hecke eigenstructure of eigenvalue \\(\\sigma\\). If \\(\\sigma\\) lies in a component \\(Z\\), then \\(M_\\sigma\\in\\mathcal C_{s,Z}\\).</p><p>The assertion is in the ind-constructible dg category and includes no boundedness, perversity, or Weil-structure assertion. The eigenvalue is the actual parameter, including its extension data, not merely its semisimplification.</p>", "proof": "<p>Put \\(A=\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\). This category satisfies Proposition 9 by [3, §7.9 and Corollary C.4.4]. On the disjoint union of components, compact generators have finite component support.</p><p>There is a continuous fully faithful \\(A\\)-linear functor\n\\[\n\\jmath_Y:\nA\\xrightarrow[\\sim]{\\Upsilon_Y}\n\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\{0\\}}(Y)\n\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y),\n\\qquad\n\\Upsilon_Y(Q)=Q\\otimes\\omega_Y\n\\]\n[12, §1.3.1, Equation (1.2)].</p><p>Apply Proposition 9 to\n\\[\na=\\sigma^*:A\\to\\operatorname{Vect}_E.\n\\]\nIts continuous right adjoint \\(\\sigma_*\\) is coherently \\(A\\)-linear and \\(\\sigma_*E\\ne0\\). Let \\(\\operatorname{Vect}_\\sigma\\) denote \\(\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\) with the action through \\(\\sigma^*\\). The composite\n\\[\n\\Phi_\\sigma:\n\\operatorname{Vect}_\\sigma\n\\xrightarrow{\\sigma_*}A\n\\xrightarrow{\\jmath_Y}\n\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y)\n\\xrightarrow{(\\mathbb L_G^{\\mathrm{restr}})^{-1}}\n\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G)\n\\]\nuses Theorem 1 and is continuous and \\(A\\)-linear. Set\n\\[\nM_\\sigma=\\Phi_\\sigma(E).\n\\]\nIt is nonzero by Proposition 9, full faithfulness of \\(\\jmath_Y\\), and the equivalence.</p><p>If \\(\\sigma\\in Z\\), then \\(\\sigma^*e_Z=E\\) and the complementary idempotent pulls back to zero. Linearity gives \\(M_\\sigma\\in\\mathcal C_{s,Z}\\).</p><p>The action of universal evaluation \\(\\mathcal E_V^I\\) is the multi-leg Hecke functor [3, §§14.2–14.3]. Extend \\(\\Phi_\\sigma\\) by the identity on \\(\\mathcal Q_I\\). Its module structure gives\n\\[\n\\mathrm H_I(V,M_\\sigma)\n\\simeq\n(\\Phi_\\sigma\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(\\sigma^I(V))\n\\simeq\nM_\\sigma\\boxtimes\\sigma^I(V).\n\\]\nThe second equivalence uses that a continuous \\(E\\)-linear functor out of \\(\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\) is tensoring with its value on \\(E\\).</p><p>Naturality, units, and tensor compatibilities follow from the coherent module structure. Permutations, collisions, and maps of finite sets follow from universal symmetric monoidal evaluation. These give the homotopy-coherent structure of [3, §15.2], equivalently the eigenobject obtained by restricting along the Ran-to-spectral action [3, §15.1]. Evaluation uses \\(\\sigma\\) itself, whether or not it is semisimple.</p>"}, {"label": "Theorem 6", "orig": "Corollary 9.5 (Conditional rational unpriming)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the rational setting and spectral-action datum of Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix finite-field descent data \\(X_0,G_0\\), and let \\(\\operatorname{Frob}\\) denote geometric Frobenius on \\(Y_0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(Y'_0\\subset Y_0\\) be a Frobenius-stable open-and-closed support union whose complementary idempotent acts by zero.</p><p class=\"li\">In addition, assume a canonical primed trace isomorphism over \\(E_0\\) is given:\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{Funct}_c(\\operatorname{Bun}_{G_0}(\\mathbb F_q),E_0)\n  \\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n  \\Gamma^{\\operatorname{IndCoh}}\n  \\bigl((Y'_0)^{\\operatorname{Frob}},\n  \\omega_{(Y'_0)^{\\operatorname{Frob}}}\\bigr).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Assume this given map intertwines the excursion and spherical Hecke actions in the same Frobenius and Satake conventions.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The same canonical formula and compatibilities hold with \\(Y_0^{\\operatorname{Frob}}\\) in place of \\((Y'_0)^{\\operatorname{Frob}}\\).</p><p>The rational primed comparison map is an additional input, not a construction of this paper.</p>", "proof": "<p>Theorem 2 identifies the actual inclusion \\(Y'_0\\subset Y_0\\) with equality. Consequently their derived Frobenius fixed-point stacks, dualizing objects, and ind-coherent global-sections functors agree. Substitute this equality in the given primed trace map. Its stated excursion and spherical Hecke compatibilities remain those of the same map.</p><p>The additional rational comparison map is not constructed here. The primed arithmetic theorem used in Theorem 4 has coefficients \\(E\\); its existence alone does not give a canonical map over \\(E_0\\). Equality of fixed-point prestacks supplies none of the additional rational Frobenius, dualizing-object, ind-coherent-global-sections, or excursion comparison data.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Specialization, a missing spectral summand, and arbitrary-leg Hecke vanishing; original Proposition 2.1 and Lemma 2.2.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Specialization, a missing spectral summand, and arbitrary-leg Hecke vanishing; original Proposition 2.1 and Lemma 2.2.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Assume the geometric setting of Theorem 1, in either regime A or regime B.</p><p class=\"li\">Initially let\n  \\[\n  R=W(k),\\qquad S=\\operatorname{Spec}R,\\qquad K=\\operatorname{Frac}R,\n  \\]\n  and fix an algebraic closure \\(\\overline K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Allow replacement of \\(R\\) by its integral closure in a finite extension of \\(K\\) inside \\(\\overline K\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal X/S\\) be a smooth projective lift of \\(X\\), let \\(G/S\\) be the split reductive lift determined by the root datum, and put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal B=\\operatorname{Bun}_G(\\mathcal X/S).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Psi_T:\\operatorname{Shv}(T_{\\overline K})\\to\\operatorname{Shv}(T_s)\\) denote geometric specialization with monodromy forgotten; no inertia invariants are taken.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_s=\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(\\mathcal B_s),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal C_{\\overline K}\n  =\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(\\mathcal B_{\\overline K}),\n  \\qquad\n  Y_{\\overline K}\n  =\\operatorname{LS}^{\\mathrm{restr}}_{\\check G}(\\mathcal X_{\\overline K}).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">The established specialization map identifies \\(Y\\) with an open-and-closed union in \\(Y_{\\overline K}\\). Define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D_Y=\n  \\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\n  \\underset{\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y_{\\overline K})}{\\otimes}\n  \\mathcal C_{\\overline K}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose a connected component \\(C\\subset Y\\) is disjoint from \\(Y'\\), and define\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal D_C=\n  \\operatorname{QCoh}(C)\n  \\underset{\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)}{\\otimes}\n  \\mathcal D_Y.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The category \\(\\mathcal D_C\\) is nonzero, every \\(D\\in\\mathcal D_C\\) satisfies \\(\\Psi_{\\mathcal B}(D)=0\\), and it contains a nonzero object \\(F\\) compact both in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\overline K}\\) and in \\(\\operatorname{Shv}(\\mathcal B_{\\overline K})\\). On every finite-type smooth scheme chart \\(b:U\\to\\mathcal B\\), \\(b_{\\overline K}^*F\\) is bounded constructible.</p><p>Moreover, if \\(a:T\\to\\mathcal B\\) is smooth, \\(T\\) is a finite-type \\(S\\)-scheme or algebraic space, and \\(\\ell_T:T\\to\\mathcal X\\) is any \\(S\\)-morphism, then for every finite-dimensional \\(V\\in\\operatorname{Rep}(\\check G)\\),\n\\[\n\\Psi_T(a_{\\overline K}^*F)=0,\n\\qquad\n\\Psi_T\\bigl((a,\\ell_T)_{\\overline K}^*\\mathrm H_V(F)\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nThe assertions also hold on the smooth locus of \\(a\\) and after the allowed finite trait replacements.</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 1.1 constructs the trait models and geometric specialization. Lemma 1.2 constructs the nonzero compact object in the missing spectral summand and proves its specialization vanishes. Lemma 1.3 supplies both chartwise and arbitrary-leg Hecke vanishings.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Trait and geometric-specialization formalism; original Section 2.1.** Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<p>**Trait and geometric-specialization formalism; original Section 2.1.**</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the geometric data and coefficient field of Proposition 1.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The lifts and specialization functors used in Proposition 1 exist. The stack \\(\\mathcal B\\) is algebraic, locally of finite presentation, has affine diagonal, and is smooth over \\(S\\).</p><p>Geometric specialization is exact, continuous, preserves constructibility, and commutes with smooth pullback and representable proper pushforward. It is unchanged by the allowed finite trait replacements. For \\(T=S\\), it is the identity on underlying geometric-point coefficient complexes.</p><p>If \\(a:T\\to\\mathcal B\\) is smooth and \\(L\\) is a finite-rank lisse sheaf on \\(T\\), then\n\\[\n\\Psi_T(a_{\\overline K}^*D\\otimes L_{\\overline K})\n\\simeq\na_s^*\\Psi_{\\mathcal B}(D)\\otimes L_s\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\nfor every \\(D\\in\\operatorname{Shv}(\\mathcal B_{\\overline K})\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Since \\(k\\) is perfect, every nonzero Witt vector is a power of \\(p\\) times a unit. Thus \\(W(k)\\) is a complete Noetherian discrete valuation ring, strictly henselian and excellent [24, Tag 07QS].</p><p>At every infinitesimal lifting step for a smooth curve, the obstruction lies in \\(H^2(X,T_X)=0\\). An ample invertible sheaf lifts because its obstruction lies in \\(H^2(X,\\mathcal O_X)=0\\). Algebraization of the resulting proper formal curve with its ample invertible sheaf gives a projective lift. It is smooth near the special fiber. Its nonsmooth locus is closed in a proper scheme over a local base and, if nonempty, would meet the special fiber. Hence the lift is smooth everywhere [24, Tags 0DZQ and 0E7R].</p><p>The split root datum gives a split reductive group over \\(\\mathbb Z\\), hence over \\(S\\) [7, Theorems 6.1.16(2) and 6.1.17]. The mapping-stack theorem [15, Theorem 1.2(i)–(iii)] applies to\n\\[\n\\mathcal B=\\operatorname{Hom}_S(\\mathcal X,B_SG):\n\\]\n\\(\\mathcal X/S\\) is proper, flat, and finitely presented, while \\(B_SG\\) is locally of finite presentation and quasi-separated, with affine stabilizers and diagonal. The obstruction to deforming a bundle lies in the second cohomology of its adjoint bundle, which vanishes on a curve. Thus \\(\\mathcal B/S\\) is smooth.</p><p>The integral closure in an allowed finite extension is a finite complete discrete valuation ring with residue field \\(k\\) [24, Tags 0EXQ and 0323]. It remains excellent and strictly henselian.</p><p>On finite-type affine charts, construct geometric nearby cycles first for finite coefficient rings at finite extensions of the generic field; pass through compatible coefficient limits, invert \\(\\ell\\), extend to \\(E\\), and extend continuously from constructibles [12, §§4.1.1–4.1.5]. Smooth compatibility gives the stack version. The stated exactness, continuity, constructibility, smooth-pullback, and proper-pushforward properties are those of [12, §4.1.6].</p><p>After a fixed finite extension, the finite subextensions of \\(\\overline K\\) containing it form a cofinal subsystem: replace a stage by its compositum with the fixed extension. The remaining models and torsion nearby-cycle functors agree, as do subsequent coefficient operations and ind-extensions. This proves invariance. On \\(S\\), specialization is the identity on geometric coefficient complexes, without taking inertia invariants.</p><p>A lisse sheaf on the source of a smooth morphism is universally locally acyclic over the target. Formula (2.1) is therefore [12, Lemma 4.1.9]; equivalently, it follows from smooth base change and the tensor formula for a lisse sheaf extending over the model. Continuity extends it to arbitrary \\(D\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "Proposition 2.1", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use \\(Y,Y',Y_{\\overline K},\\mathcal C_s,\\mathcal C_{\\overline K},\\mathcal D_Y,\\mathcal D_C\\) of Proposition 1, and assume \\(C\\cap Y'=\\varnothing\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The category \\(\\mathcal D_C\\) is nonzero, every \\(D\\in\\mathcal D_C\\) has \\(\\Psi_{\\mathcal B}(D)=0\\), and there is a nonzero \\(F\\in\\mathcal D_C\\) compact in both \\(\\mathcal C_{\\overline K}\\) and the ambient sheaf category. Its pullbacks to finite-type smooth scheme charts are bounded constructible.</p>", "proof": "<p>Restriction gives an equivalence\n\\[\n\\operatorname{QLisse}(\\mathcal X)\\simeq\\operatorname{QLisse}(X)\n\\]\nand, by restriction to the geometric generic fiber, a symmetric monoidal functor\n\\[\n\\rho:\\operatorname{QLisse}(X)\\to\n\\operatorname{QLisse}(\\mathcal X_{\\overline K}).\n\\]\nThe induced \\(i:Y\\hookrightarrow Y_{\\overline K}\\) is an open-and-closed union of components [12, §3.1.3], so \\(\\mathcal D_Y\\) is a direct summand of \\(\\mathcal C_{\\overline K}\\).</p><p>By [12, Corollary 4.3.6 and §4.3.9], actual chartwise specialization restricts to\n\\[\n\\Psi_{\\mathcal B}:\\mathcal D_Y\\to\\mathcal C_s.\n\\]\nIt is \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\)-linear [12, Theorem 3.1.6(B) and §4.3.10]. The characteristic-zero equivalence identifies\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_Y\\simeq\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y).\n\\tag{2.3}\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(e_C\\) be \\(\\mathcal O_C\\) extended by zero. It projects to \\(\\mathcal D_C\\) and acts by zero on \\(\\mathcal C_s\\), since the given primed equivalence is spectral-linear and \\(C\\cap Y'=\\varnothing\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\Psi_{\\mathcal B}(D)\n\\simeq\\Psi_{\\mathcal B}(e_C\\cdot D)\n\\simeq e_C\\cdot\\Psi_{\\mathcal B}(D)=0.\n\\]\nUnder (2.3), \\(\\mathcal D_C\\) is \\(\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(C)\\), which is nonzero: the fully faithful functor from \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(C)\\) sends the nonzero \\(\\mathcal O_C\\) to a nonzero object [12, §§1.3.1–1.3.4].</p><p>The category \\(\\mathcal D_Y\\) is compactly generated [12, §3.1.7]. In the binary decomposition\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_Y\\simeq\\mathcal D_C\\times\\mathcal D_{Y\\setminus C},\n\\]\nprojection preserves compactness because its right adjoint, inclusion of the factor, is continuous. At least one projected compact generator is nonzero. Choose it as \\(F\\).</p><p>Inclusion of either direct factor preserves compactness, since its right adjoint is the continuous projection. Applying this to\n\\(\\mathcal D_C\\subset\\mathcal D_Y\\subset\\mathcal C_{\\overline K}\\)\nshows compactness in \\(\\mathcal C_{\\overline K}\\). The nilpotent inclusion into the ambient sheaf category preserves compactness [12, Theorem 1.1.7]. A compact sheaf on an algebraic stack is constructible on finite-type smooth charts [3, §F.2.2].</p><p>Only a binary direct-factor decomposition is used; neither finiteness of the component set nor compactness of \\(e_C\\) is required.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "Lemma 2.2 (Hecke vanishing with an arbitrary leg)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(F\\) be supplied by Lemma 1.2. Let \\(a:T\\to\\mathcal B\\) be smooth, where \\(T\\) is a finite-type \\(S\\)-scheme or algebraic space, and let \\(\\ell_T:T\\to\\mathcal X\\) be arbitrary.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For every finite-dimensional representation \\(V\\) of \\(\\check G\\),\n\\[\n\\Psi_T(a_{\\overline K}^*F)=0,\n\\qquad\n\\Psi_T\\bigl((a,\\ell_T)_{\\overline K}^*\\mathrm H_V(F)\\bigr)=0.\n\\]\nThe same holds on the smooth locus of \\(a\\) and after allowed finite trait replacements.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{V,s}\\in\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\otimes\\operatorname{QLisse}(X),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal E_{V,\\overline K}\\in\n\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y_{\\overline K})\\otimes\n\\operatorname{QLisse}(\\mathcal X_{\\overline K})\n\\]\nbe universal evaluation. By definition of \\(i\\),\n\\[\n(i^*\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(\\mathcal E_{V,\\overline K})\n\\simeq\n(\\operatorname{id}\\otimes\\rho)(\\mathcal E_{V,s}).\n\\tag{2.4}\n\\]\nThis holds on every affine test mapping to \\(Y\\). To pass to the global identity, use compact generation and dualizability of \\(\\operatorname{QLisse}(X)\\) [3, §1.2.11 and §§E.2.6–E.2.7]: tensoring with it preserves the limit defining \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\).</p><p>A distinct input is\n\\[\n\\mathcal E_{V,s}\\in\n\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\otimes\\operatorname{IndLisse}(X)\n\\tag{2.5}\n\\]\n[3, Remark 14.3.9], where\n\\(\\operatorname{IndLisse}(X)=\\operatorname{Ind}(\\operatorname{Lisse}(X))\\).\nIt is not being identified with \\(\\operatorname{QLisse}(X)\\); that identification fails for \\(X=\\mathbb P^1\\).</p><p>Apply the spectral action in (2.5) to \\(F\\), then apply \\(\\operatorname{id}\\otimes\\rho\\) and exterior product. By (2.4), the result is \\(\\mathrm H_V(F)\\). Elementary tensors generate categorical tensor products under colimits, and bounded truncations build every constructible lisse complex from finite-rank lisse sheaves. Hence \\(\\mathrm H_V(F)\\) belongs to the stable colimit closure of\n\\[\nD\\boxtimes L_{\\overline K},\\qquad D\\in\\mathcal D_C,\n\\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nwhere \\(L_{\\overline K}\\) extends to a finite-rank lisse \\(L_S\\) on \\(\\mathcal X\\). The extension follows from proper henselian invariance of finite étale covers [24, Tag 0A48], applied to compatible finite coefficient local systems of a lisse lattice, followed by inversion of \\(\\ell\\) and coefficient extension.</p><p>The first vanishing follows from smooth base change and Lemma 1.2. For (2.6),\n\\[\n(a,\\ell_T)_{\\overline K}^*(D\\boxtimes L_{\\overline K})\n=\na_{\\overline K}^*D\\otimes\n\\ell_{T,\\overline K}^*L_{\\overline K}.\n\\]\nThe sheaf \\(\\ell_T^*L_S\\) is lisse regardless of smoothness of \\(\\ell_T\\). Formula (2.1) gives\n\\[\n\\Psi_T\\bigl(a_{\\overline K}^*D\\otimes\n\\ell_{T,\\overline K}^*L_{\\overline K}\\bigr)\n\\simeq\na_s^*\\Psi_{\\mathcal B}(D)\\otimes\\ell_{T,s}^*L_s=0.\n\\]\nPullback and specialization are continuous, so the same holds for the stable colimit closure. Locality on the smooth locus and finite-extension invariance follow from Lemma 1.1. No smooth-base-change assertion for the combined map \\((a,\\ell_T)\\) is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "Proposition 3.1 (A Levi reduction for a formal Higgs field)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(k,G,\\kappa\\) satisfy Hypothesis 1.1 and either regime A or regime B of Theorem 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Write \\(\\chi:\\mathfrak g\\to\\mathfrak g/\\!/G\\) for the adjoint quotient.</p><p class=\"li\">For a cocharacter \\(\\lambda:\\mathbb G_m\\to G\\), let \\(d\\lambda\\in\\mathfrak g\\) be the image of \\(1\\in\\operatorname{Lie}(\\mathbb G_m)=k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a(t)\\in\\mathfrak g[[t]]\\) satisfy \\(\\chi(a(0))\\ne\\chi(0)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a change of frame by an element of \\(G(k[[t]])\\), there are a Levi subgroup \\(M\\subset G\\), with \\(\\mathfrak m=\\operatorname{Lie}(M)\\), and an actual cocharacter \\(\\lambda:\\mathbb G_m\\to Z(M)\\) such that\n\\[\na(t)\\in\\mathfrak m[[t]],\\qquad\n\\kappa(a(0),d\\lambda)\\ne0,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\operatorname{ad}_{a(0)}:\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m\n\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m\n\\]\nis invertible.</p><p>The case \\(M=G\\), including invertibility on the zero vector space, is allowed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a closed faithful representation. Its differential identifies \\(\\mathfrak g\\) with a restricted Lie subalgebra of \\(\\operatorname{End}(V)\\), with restricted operation equal to matrix \\(p\\)-th power.</p><p>**Regime A.** Write \\(a_0=a(0)=s+n\\) for matrix Jordan decomposition. Its semisimple eigenvalues lie in a finite field \\(\\mathbb F_{p^h}\\). Choose \\(e\\) divisible by \\(h\\) and large enough that \\(n^{p^e}=0\\). Then\n\\[\na_0^{[p^e]}=a_0^{p^e}=s^{p^e}+n^{p^e}=s,\n\\]\nso \\(s,n\\in\\mathfrak g\\). Lemma 2.2 supplies \\(s\\in\\mathfrak t\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), \\(M=Z_G(s)\\), and quotient invertibility. Since \\(s\\in\\mathfrak z(\\mathfrak m)\\), where \\(\\kappa\\) is nondegenerate, Lemma 2.3 supplies an actual central cocharacter \\(\\lambda\\) with\n\\(\\kappa(s,d\\lambda)\\ne0\\). Equation (3.3) gives the required pairing with \\(a_0\\). No Weyl-order assumption is used.</p><p>**Regime B.** Again write \\(a_0=s+n\\) and choose \\(e\\) with \\(n^{p^e}=0\\). Let \\(V_0\\subset k\\) be the finite-dimensional \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-span of the eigenvalues of \\(s\\), and let\n\\[\nV_e=\\{v^{p^e}:v\\in V_0\\}.\n\\]\nThe inverse of \\(p^e\\)-Frobenius \\(V_e\\to V_0\\) is \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-linear. If \\(V_0=0\\), then \\(a_0=n\\), contrary to the argument of Lemma 2.2 and \\(\\chi(a_0)\\ne\\chi(0)\\).</p><p>For an \\(\\mathbb F_p\\)-basis \\(\\beta_1,\\ldots,\\beta_r\\) of \\(V_e\\), the Moore matrix\n\\[\n(\\beta_i^{p^j})_{1\\le i\\le r,\\ 0\\le j&lt;r}\n\\]\nis invertible. Otherwise a nonzero additive polynomial of degree at most \\(p^{r-1}\\) would vanish on the \\(p^r\\) elements of \\(V_e\\). Hence an additive polynomial\n\\[\nQ(z)=\\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}c_jz^{p^j}\n\\]\nagrees with inverse Frobenius on \\(V_e\\), and\n\\[\ns=Q(a_0^{p^e})\n=\\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}c_ja_0^{[p^{e+j}]}\\in\\mathfrak g.\n\\]\nThus \\(n\\in\\mathfrak g\\), and Lemma 2.2 again gives \\(s,M\\), and invertibility.</p><p>Let \\(h_M=|W_M|\\), invertible because \\(W_M\\subset W_G\\). The operator\n\\[\ne_M=\\frac1{h_M}\\sum_{w\\in W_M}w:\\mathfrak t\\to\\mathfrak t\n\\]\nis a self-adjoint projection. Thus \\(\\kappa\\) is nondegenerate on \\(\\mathfrak t^{W_M}\\), which contains \\(s\\). The differential reflection formula\n\\[\ns_\\alpha(h)=h-d\\alpha(h)d\\alpha^\\vee\n\\]\nand Lemma 2.1 identify \\(\\mathfrak t^{W_M}=\\mathfrak z(\\mathfrak m)\\).</p><p>For a basis \\(\\mu_i\\) of \\(\\Lambda\\), form integral sums\n\\[\n\\nu_i=\\sum_{w\\in W_M}w\\mu_i\\in\\Lambda^{W_M}.\n\\]\nTheir differentials span \\(\\mathfrak t^{W_M}\\), since\n\\(d\\nu_i=h_Me_M(d\\mu_i)\\).\nThe integral reflection formula and torsion-freeness give\n\\[\n\\Lambda^{W_M}=X_*(Z(M)).\n\\]\nNo division of a cocharacter by \\(h_M\\) is made. Nondegeneracy gives \\(\\lambda\\) with nonzero pairing, and (3.3) gives the pairing with \\(a_0\\).</p><p>In either regime, apply Lemma 2.4 after constant conjugation. The formal change fixes \\(a_0\\), so all required assertions hold.</p><p>As in original Remark 3.6, centrality makes\n\\(\\operatorname{Ad}(\\lambda(t))a(t)=a(t)\\).\nEvery nonzero \\(\\lambda\\)-weight space injects into \\(\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m\\); equality \\(Z_G(\\lambda)=M\\) is not required. Central modifications, zero-dimensional Hecke orbits, and tori remain included.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "Lemma 3.2 (Root pairings and Borel conjugacy)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Let \\(T\\subset G\\) be a maximal torus, put\n\\[\n\\Lambda=X_*(T),\\qquad \\Lambda^\\vee=X^*(T),\n\\qquad \\mathfrak t=\\Lambda\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}k,\n\\]\nand retain the invariant form of Proposition 2. For a root \\(\\alpha\\), write \\(d\\alpha:\\mathfrak t\\to k\\) and \\(d\\alpha^\\vee\\in\\mathfrak t\\) for the differentiated root and coroot.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The restriction of \\(\\kappa\\) to \\(\\mathfrak t\\) is nondegenerate. For every root \\(\\alpha\\), there is \\(c_\\alpha\\in k^\\times\\) with\n\\[\n\\kappa(d\\alpha^\\vee,h)=c_\\alpha d\\alpha(h)\n\\qquad(h\\in\\mathfrak t).\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nNeither \\(d\\alpha\\) nor \\(d\\alpha^\\vee\\) vanishes. Every element of \\(\\mathfrak g\\) is conjugate into the Lie algebra of a Borel subgroup containing \\(T\\), and every semisimple element is conjugate into \\(\\mathfrak t\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The \\(T\\)-weight decomposition is\n\\[\n\\mathfrak g=\\mathfrak t\\oplus\\bigoplus_\\alpha\\mathfrak g_\\alpha.\n\\]\nInvariance under \\(T\\) makes \\(\\mathfrak t\\) orthogonal to root spaces and pairs \\(\\mathfrak g_\\alpha\\) only with \\(\\mathfrak g_{-\\alpha}\\). Nondegeneracy therefore holds on \\(\\mathfrak t\\) and every opposite-root pairing.</p><p>Choose compatible root vectors with\n\\([e_\\alpha,e_{-\\alpha}]=d\\alpha^\\vee\\), obtained by differentiating the rank-one root homomorphism from \\(\\mathrm{SL}_2\\); this is valid for every isogeny type [23, §§8.1.1–8.1.4; 7, Corollary 5.1.12, Equation (5.1.4)]. Invariance yields (3.2), with\n\\(c_\\alpha=\\kappa(e_\\alpha,e_{-\\alpha})\\ne0\\).\nThus one differential vanishes exactly when the other does. Simultaneous vanishing would imply\n\\(\\alpha\\in p\\Lambda^\\vee\\) and \\(\\alpha^\\vee\\in p\\Lambda\\), contradicting\n\\(\\langle\\alpha,\\alpha^\\vee\\rangle=2\\), since \\(p^2\\nmid2\\). This uses torus characters, not only differentials, and also applies in characteristic two.</p><p>For a Borel \\(B\\supset T\\), the incidence map\n\\[\nG\\times^B\\mathfrak b\\longrightarrow\\mathfrak g\n\\]\nis proper because its source is closed in \\((G/B)\\times\\mathfrak g\\). Choose \\(c\\in\\mathfrak t\\) outside all root-differential hyperplanes. At \\([1,c]\\), negative-root tangent directions map isomorphically to the negative-root spaces and \\(\\mathfrak b\\) supplies the other directions. The map is dominant, hence surjective.</p><p>For a semisimple element in \\(\\mathfrak b\\), write it \\(c+u\\), with \\(u\\) positive-root. Conjugate by positive root groups in increasing root height. When \\(d\\alpha(c)\\ne0\\), the \\(\\alpha\\)-root group changes that coefficient by \\(-z\\,d\\alpha(c)\\), changing other terms only in greater height. Eliminate these coefficients. The result is \\(c+u_0\\) with \\([c,u_0]=0\\). In a faithful representation, \\(c\\) is semisimple and \\(u_0\\) nilpotent; matrix Jordan decomposition forces \\(u_0=0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.2", "orig": "Lemma 3.3 (Consequences of Jordan parts in \\(\\mathfrak g\\))", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain Hypothesis 1.1 and a closed faithful representation \\(G\\hookrightarrow\\mathrm{GL}(V)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  a_0=s+n\\in\\mathfrak g,\n  \\qquad s,n\\in\\mathfrak g,\\qquad [s,n]=0,\n  \\]\n  with \\(s\\) semisimple and \\(n\\) nilpotent in that representation.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\chi(a_0)\\ne\\chi(0)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After conjugation, \\(s\\in\\mathfrak t\\setminus\\{0\\}\\), \\(M=Z_G(s)\\) is a Levi subgroup, \\(a_0\\in\\mathfrak m\\), and \\(\\operatorname{ad}_{a_0}\\) is invertible on \\(\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m\\). For every \\(\\lambda:\\mathbb G_m\\to Z(M)\\),\n\\[\n\\kappa(a_0,d\\lambda)=\\kappa(s,d\\lambda).\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Lemma 2.1 conjugates \\(s\\) into \\(\\mathfrak t\\). If \\(s=0\\), conjugate \\(n\\) into a Borel Lie algebra. Projection to \\(\\mathfrak t\\) preserves restricted \\(p\\)-operations. Since \\(n\\) is killed by an iterated \\(p\\)-operation and that operation is injective on \\(\\mathfrak t\\), its toral projection is zero. A strictly dominant cocharacter contracts the positive-root Lie algebra to zero, so every invariant polynomial takes the same value on \\(n\\) and zero, a contradiction.</p><p>Hypothesis 1.1 makes \\(M=Z_G(s)\\) a Levi. Its Lie algebra is \\(\\ker\\operatorname{ad}_s\\), and contains \\(a_0\\). On \\(\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m\\), \\(\\operatorname{ad}_s\\) is diagonalizable with nonzero eigenvalues. The commuting \\(\\operatorname{ad}_n\\) is nilpotent, since\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{ad}_n)^{p^e}\n=\\operatorname{ad}_{n^{[p^e]}}=0\n\\]\nfor large \\(e\\). Their sum is invertible on each \\(\\operatorname{ad}_s\\)-eigenspace.</p><p>For (3.3), conjugate \\(n\\) inside \\(M\\) into a Borel Lie algebra containing \\(T\\). The incidence argument of Lemma 2.1 applies to \\(M\\): its roots have nonzero differentials, and the same weight decomposition makes \\(\\kappa\\) nondegenerate on \\(\\mathfrak m\\). Restricted nilpotence again kills the toral projection. The resulting positive-root element is orthogonal to \\(\\mathfrak t\\). Since \\(d\\lambda\\) is fixed by \\(M\\), invariance gives \\(\\kappa(n,d\\lambda)=0\\) before conjugation as well.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.3", "orig": "Lemma 3.4 (Central cocharacters from the invariant form)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Assume the invariant-form and Levi-Lie-center nondegeneracy conditions of Hypothesis 1.1. Let \\(M\\supset T\\) be any Levi subgroup, with \\(\\mathfrak m=\\operatorname{Lie}(M)\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every Levi Cartan determinant is prime to \\(p\\), and\n\\[\n\\operatorname{span}_k\n\\{d\\lambda:\\lambda\\in X_*(Z(M))\\}\n=\\mathfrak z(\\mathfrak m).\n\\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nHere \\(X_*(Z(M))\\) consists of actual cocharacters of the group center, viewed in \\(X_*(T)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose simple roots \\(I=\\{\\alpha_1,\\ldots,\\alpha_r\\}\\) of \\(M\\) and put\n\\[\nJ_I=\\operatorname{span}_k\\{d\\alpha_i^\\vee\\}\\subset\\mathfrak t.\n\\]\nNonzero root differentials imply that a central element of \\(\\mathfrak m\\) has no root-space component. Equation (3.2) identifies\n\\[\n\\mathfrak z(\\mathfrak m)\n=\\bigcap_i\\ker(d\\alpha_i)=J_I^\\perp.\n\\]\nThe form is nondegenerate on this center and on \\(\\mathfrak t\\), hence on \\(J_I\\).</p><p>Define integral maps\n\\[\nU:\\Lambda\\to\\mathbb Z^r,\\quad\ny\\mapsto(\\langle\\alpha_i,y\\rangle)_i,\n\\qquad\nV:\\mathbb Z^r\\to\\Lambda,\\quad\ne_j\\mapsto\\alpha_j^\\vee.\n\\]\nThen \\(C_I=UV\\) is the Cartan matrix. Reduction to \\(k\\) gives\n\\[\nV_k^*\\kappa=DU_k,\\qquad\nD=\\operatorname{diag}(c_{\\alpha_1},\\ldots,c_{\\alpha_r}).\n\\]\nThe radical of \\(V_k^*\\kappa V_k\\) is \\(\\ker V_k\\), by nondegeneracy on \\(\\operatorname{im}V_k\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\operatorname{rank}(C_{I,k})\n=\\operatorname{rank}(V_k)\n=\\operatorname{rank}(U_k).\n\\tag{3.5}\n\\]</p><p>If a Levi Cartan determinant were divisible by \\(p\\), the determinant list\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|ccccccccc}\n\\text{type}&amp;A_r&amp;B_r&amp;C_r&amp;D_r&amp;E_6&amp;E_7&amp;E_8&amp;F_4&amp;G_2\\\\\n\\hline\n\\det&amp;r+1&amp;2&amp;2&amp;4&amp;3&amp;2&amp;1&amp;1&amp;1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\nwould yield a further Levi of type \\(A_{p-1}\\): take \\(p-1\\) consecutive vertices in type \\(A\\), one vertex in the determinant-two or determinant-four cases, or two adjacent vertices in \\(E_6\\) when \\(p=3\\) [19, Theorem C.56 and §24(c)].</p><p>For that \\(A_{p-1}\\) Levi, \\(\\det C_I=p\\). By (3.5), both reductions \\(U_k,V_k\\) have rank less than \\(r=p-1\\). Every maximal minor of each integral matrix is divisible by \\(p\\). Cauchy–Binet gives\n\\[\n\\det C_I\n=\n\\sum_{\\substack{J\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,N_T\\}\\\\|J|=r}}\n\\det(U_{:,J})\\det(V_{J,:}),\n\\]\ndivisible by \\(p^2\\), a contradiction. The full lattices retain central torus directions and make no isogeny-type assumption.</p><p>For arbitrary \\(M\\), let \\(d_I=\\det C_I\\) and\n\\[\n\\Pi_I=d_I\\operatorname{id}_\\Lambda\n-\nV\\operatorname{adj}(C_I)U.\n\\]\nThen \\(U\\Pi_I=0\\). The lattice \\(\\ker U\\) is exactly \\(X_*(Z(M))\\), because its cocharacters commute with \\(T\\) and every root group of \\(M\\). On \\(\\ker U_k\\),\n\\(\\Pi_{I,k}=d_I\\operatorname{id}\\).\nSince \\(d_I\\) is invertible in \\(k\\), differentials of \\(\\ker U\\) span\n\\(\\ker U_k=\\mathfrak z(\\mathfrak m)\\).\nFor \\(r=0\\), use \\(d_I=1\\) and \\(\\Pi_I=\\operatorname{id}\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.4", "orig": "Lemma 3.5 (A single formal reduction)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G\\) be connected reductive over an algebraically closed field \\(k\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M\\subset G\\) be a Levi subgroup.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a(t)\\in\\mathfrak g[[t]]\\) have \\(a_0=a(0)\\in\\mathfrak m\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(\\operatorname{ad}_{a_0}\\) is invertible on \\(\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There exists \\(g(t)\\in G(k[[t]])\\), with \\(g(0)=1\\), such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Ad}(g(t))a(t)\\in\\mathfrak m[[t]].\n\\]\nThis statement does not require finite-field descent or a Weyl-group-order assumption.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose the current arc lies in \\(\\mathfrak m[[t]]\\) modulo \\(t^r\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), and let \\(c_r\\in\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m\\) be its \\(t^r\\)-coefficient. Choose \\(D\\in\\mathfrak g\\) with\n\\[\nc_r+[D,a_0]=0\n\\quad\\text{in }\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m.\n\\]\nSmoothness gives \\(g_r(t)\\in G(k[[t]])\\) with\n\\[\ng_r(t)\\equiv1+t^rD\\pmod{t^{r+1}}.\n\\]\nThis specifies a point in the tangent kernel for the square-zero ideal\n\\((t^r)/(t^{r+1})\\); formal smoothness lifts it successively to higher order. Conjugation changes the \\(t^r\\)-coefficient by \\([D,a_0]\\), leaving lower coefficients unchanged.</p><p>Iterate. Since \\(g_r(t)\\equiv1\\pmod{t^r}\\), the successive products converge \\(t\\)-adically. The affine group \\(G\\) turns the compatible points modulo \\(t^j\\) into \\(g(t)\\in G(k[[t]])\\), with \\(g(0)=1\\), giving the required conjugation. No exponential series is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "Proposition 4.2 (Transverse modification)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work over the trait and bundle stack of Proposition 1, with the characteristic assumptions of Proposition 2.</p><p class=\"li\">A one-point Hecke modification is \\((P,P',x,\\alpha)\\), with \\(\\alpha\\) identifying the bundles away from the graph of \\(x\\). Write\n  \\[\n  p(P,P',x,\\alpha)=P,\\quad o(P,P',x,\\alpha)=P',\n  \\quad\\ell_H(P,P',x,\\alpha)=x.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Modification by \\(\\lambda\\) means that, in a formal input frame with coordinate \\(t\\), the output frame is \\(\\lambda(t)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(H=H^\\lambda\\) be the exact-position correspondence, let\n  \\[\n  \\mu=\\lambda^+,\\quad m=\\langle2\\rho,\\mu\\rangle,\\quad\n  \\mu^\\vee=-w_0\\mu,\\quad\\mathcal Z=\\mathcal B\\times_S\\mathcal X,\n  \\]\n  and set \\(\\widetilde p=(p,\\ell_H)\\), \\(q=(o,\\ell_H)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(P\\in\\mathcal B_s(k)\\), deformation theory, Serre duality, and \\(\\kappa\\) identify\n  \\[\n  T_P^*\\mathcal B_s\n  =\n  H^1(X,\\mathfrak g_P)^*\n  =\n  H^0(X,\\mathfrak g_P\\otimes\\omega_X).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A\\in T_P^*\\mathcal B_s\\) be non-nilpotent.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(x\\in X(k)\\), a cocharacter \\(\\lambda\\), a modification \\(h:P\\dashrightarrow P'\\), and\n\\[\n\\theta=(A',\\xi)\\in T_{(P',x)}^*(\\mathcal B_s\\times X),\n\\qquad \\xi\\ne0,\n\\]\nsuch that \\(A'=A\\) away from \\(x\\) and\n\\[\ndp_h^*A=dq_h^*\\theta.\n\\tag{4.2}\n\\]</p><p>Retain fixed-bundle identifications in the fibers\n\\[\nH^{\\mathrm{in}}_{P,x}=\\widetilde p^{-1}(P,x),\n\\qquad\nH^{\\mathrm{out}}_{P',x}=q^{-1}(P',x).\n\\]\nOn the first fiber, restrict \\(p^*A\\) to the \\(q\\)-relative tangent bundle to obtain \\(S_A\\). On the second, restrict \\(o^*A'\\) to the \\(\\widetilde p\\)-relative tangent bundle to obtain \\(S_{A'}\\). Both vanish at \\(h\\), and\n\\[\n(dS_A)_h:\nT_hH^{\\mathrm{in}}_{P,x}\n\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n(T_hH^{\\mathrm{out}}_{P',x})^*,\n\\]\n\\[\n(dS_{A'})_h:\nT_hH^{\\mathrm{out}}_{P',x}\n\\xrightarrow{\\sim}\n(T_hH^{\\mathrm{in}}_{P,x})^*\n\\]\nare isomorphisms. Each section has an isolated reduced zero at \\(h\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(x\\) where the value of \\(A\\) is non-nilpotent, and write\n\\[\nA=a(t)\\,dt\n\\]\nin a formal coordinate and frame. Chevalley restriction identifies the nilpotent fiber with \\(\\chi^{-1}(\\chi(0))\\), so Proposition 2 applies. Obtain\n\\[\na(t)\\in\\mathfrak m[[t]],\\qquad\n\\operatorname{ad}_{a(0)}\\text{ invertible on }\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m,\n\\qquad\n\\kappa(a(0),d\\lambda)\\ne0.\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\nAfter constant conjugation, \\(\\lambda\\) labels the integral model of Lemma 3.1.</p><p>Modify by \\(\\lambda(t)\\). Since \\(\\lambda\\) centralizes \\(M\\), the transported field remains regular with expression \\(a(t)\\,dt\\). Glue it to \\(A\\) away from \\(x\\) to obtain \\(A'\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nL=\\mathfrak g[[t]],\\qquad\nL'=\\operatorname{Ad}(\\lambda(t))L,\\qquad I=L\\cap L'.\n\\]\nFor the algebraic \\(\\lambda\\)-weight spaces \\(\\mathfrak g_j\\), with integer weights not reduced modulo \\(p\\),\n\\[\nL'=\\bigoplus_j t^j\\mathfrak g_j[[t]],\n\\]\n\\[\nP_1=L/I=\n\\bigoplus_{j&gt;0}\\mathfrak g_j\\otimes_k k[[t]]/(t^j),\n\\]\n\\[\nP_2=L'/I=\n\\bigoplus_{j&lt;0}\\mathfrak g_j\\otimes_k\n(t^jk[[t]]/k[[t]]).\n\\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nInfinitesimal disc automorphisms give the fixed-side tangents\n\\[\nT_hH^{\\mathrm{in}}_{P,x}=P_1,\\qquad\nT_hH^{\\mathrm{out}}_{P',x}=P_2.\n\\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nTheir stabilizer tangent is \\(I\\), and the smooth orbit has dimension \\(m\\), so these are the full tangent spaces.</p><p>The residue pairing\n\\[\n\\beta:P_1\\times P_2\\to k,\\qquad\n\\beta(D,C)=\\operatorname{Res}_{t=0}\\kappa(D,C)\\,dt\n\\tag{4.6}\n\\]\nis perfect. Both \\(L,L'\\) are self-annihilating. Explicitly, \\(\\mathfrak g_j\\) pairs perfectly with \\(\\mathfrak g_{-j}\\), and \\(t^a\\), \\(0\\le a&lt;j\\), pairs with \\(t^{-a-1}\\). This includes weights divisible by \\(p\\).</p><p>Choose the gluing sign in which \\(1+\\epsilon C\\) gives the boundary class of principal part \\(C\\), paired with \\(A\\) by the residue. For output-fixed \\(C\\in L'\\), this residue vanishes because \\(a\\in L'\\). Hence \\(p^*A\\) vanishes in the \\(q\\)-relative cotangent and equals \\(q^*\\theta\\) for a unique \\(\\theta\\).</p><p>Its bundle component is \\(A'\\): vary transitions at a point \\(x'\\ne x\\). Principal parts there surject onto \\(H^1(X,\\mathfrak g_{P'})\\), since \\(H^1(X,\\mathfrak g_{P'}(Nx'))=0\\) for large \\(N\\). The pairings agree because \\(A=A'\\) near \\(x'\\).</p><p>Keep the input fixed and move the leg to \\(t=\\epsilon\\). The output transition is \\(g_\\epsilon=\\lambda(t-\\epsilon)\\), with\n\\[\n(\\delta g)g^{-1}=-\\frac{d\\lambda}{t}.\n\\]\nThe cotangent identity gives\n\\[\n0=-\\kappa(a(0),d\\lambda)+\\xi(\\partial_t),\n\\qquad\n\\xi=\\kappa(a(0),d\\lambda)\\,dt|_x\\ne0.\n\\]\nThis is [3, Equation (20.20)]. Reversing the gluing sign reverses the common sign only.</p><p>For the first derivative, vary \\(L'\\) to\n\\[\nL'_\\epsilon=\\operatorname{Ad}(1+\\epsilon D)L',\n\\qquad D\\in L,\n\\]\nand extend \\(C\\in L'\\) as \\(\\operatorname{Ad}(1+\\epsilon D)C\\). Differentiation gives\n\\[\n\\left\\langle(dS_A)_h(D\\bmod I),C\\bmod I\\right\\rangle\n=\n\\operatorname{Res}\\kappa(a,[D,C])\\,dt\n=\n\\beta(\\operatorname{ad}(a)D,C).\n\\tag{4.7}\n\\]\nSince \\(S_A(h)=0\\), the derivative is independent of the extension. Preservation of \\(L,L',I\\) makes it independent of representatives.</p><p>The operator \\(\\operatorname{ad}(a(t))\\) preserves the weight summands. Every nonzero weight space injects as an invariant summand into \\(\\mathfrak g/\\mathfrak m\\), so its constant-term operator is invertible by (4.3). Its determinant over \\(k[[t]]\\) is a unit, hence it is invertible on each truncated summand of \\(P_1,P_2\\). Equation (4.7) and residue duality prove the first derivative isomorphism.</p><p>For the reverse derivative, keep output fixed, vary input by \\(C\\in L'\\), and extend \\(D\\in L\\) by \\(\\operatorname{Ad}(1+\\epsilon C)D\\). Then\n\\[\n\\left\\langle(dS_{A'})_h(C\\bmod I),D\\bmod I\\right\\rangle\n=\n\\operatorname{Res}\\kappa(a,[C,D])\\,dt\n=\n\\operatorname{Res}\\kappa([a,C],D)\\,dt.\n\\]\nInvertibility on \\(P_2\\) and residue duality give the second isomorphism. Rank-\\(m\\) sections on smooth \\(m\\)-dimensional fibers with invertible derivatives have reduced isolated zeros.</p><p>For \\(m=0\\), both derivatives are isomorphisms between zero spaces; the fibers are already smooth zero-dimensional, and the moving-leg calculation still supplies \\(\\xi\\ne0\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 3.1", "orig": "Lemma 4.1 (Hecke models and kernels)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>Use the trait, relative-position convention, and notation \\(H,\\widetilde p,q,\\mathcal Z,m,\\mu^\\vee\\) of Proposition 3.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Both \\(\\widetilde p\\) and \\(q\\) are representable, separated, and smooth of relative dimension \\(m\\). The correspondence \\(H\\) is open in a bounded correspondence \\(\\overline H\\) whose two projections to \\(\\mathcal Z\\) are representable and proper. The output-side bound has dominant label \\(\\mu^\\vee\\).</p><p>For the irreducible dual-group representation \\(V\\) corresponding to the bound and the stated convention,\n\\[\n\\mathrm H_V(F)=\\overline q_{\\overline K,*}\\mathcal I_{\\overline K}.\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\nOn \\(H_{\\overline K}\\), \\(\\mathcal I_{\\overline K}\\) is \\(p_{\\overline K}^*F\\) tensored with a fixed invertible constant complex. On a fixed-leg affine-Grassmannian fiber, its perverse intersection-complex normalization is \\(E[m]\\), with a constant Tate line if weight normalization is used.</p>", "proof": "<p>Use the split integral affine Grassmannian with loops \\(G(A((t)))\\) and positive loops \\(G(A[[t]])\\). Its integral Schubert bound is projective and its positive-loop orbit is smooth, open, fiberwise dense, and of relative dimension \\(m\\) [22, §§1.2 and 2.1]. Base change these models from \\(\\mathbb Z\\). The parameter \\(t\\) is the curve parameter even over a mixed-characteristic trait.</p><p>A coordinate along the graph of \\(x\\) and a disc frame identify fixed-input modifications with these Grassmannian spaces. The descriptions glue by Beauville–Laszlo descent [4]. The graph is an effective Cartier divisor; gluing modules also glues the coordinate algebra, multiplication, and coaction of an affine \\(G\\)-torsor, and the torsor identity is checked on the gluing pieces. Disc frames exist locally by successive smooth lifting. Changes of frame and formal coordinate preserve orbit and bound. The input-side properties therefore descend.</p><p>Swapping the bundles and inverting \\(\\alpha\\) is an involution of the Hecke correspondence. It replaces the double-coset label \\(\\mu\\) by \\(-\\mu\\), with dominant representative \\(\\mu^\\vee\\), and preserves \\(m\\). This gives the output-side assertions. Separatedness follows from the proper bound. No inversion map on the right-quotient affine Grassmannian is asserted.</p><p>Fibers retain identifications with the specified fixed bundle. An automorphism of the moving bundle compatible with the identification off the graph is the identity; there is no further quotient by fixed-bundle automorphisms.</p><p>Geometric Satake supplies the intersection complex of the bound, whose restriction to the smooth open orbit is shifted constant [21, Proposition 4.1]. The Hecke construction tensors this kernel with ordinary \\(p^*F\\) and pushes along the proper output-and-leg map [12, §§4.2.1–4.2.4]. Relabel by \\(\\mu^\\vee\\) when needed. Overall invertible shifts or Tate lines do not affect vanishing. No boundary-kernel formula is claimed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "Proposition 5.1 (Vanishing at the quadratic vertex)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work in either regime of Theorem 1 over the trait of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be the nonzero compact object of Proposition 1, with its chartwise and arbitrary-leg Hecke vanishings.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b:U\\to\\mathcal B\\) be a finite-type smooth scheme chart, \\(f\\in\\Gamma(U,\\mathcal O_U)\\), and \\(u\\in U_s(k)\\), with \\(f(u)=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume\n  \\[\n  d(f_s)_u=b^*A\n  \\]\n  for a non-nilpotent \\(A\\in T^*_{b(u)}\\mathcal B_s\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put \\(F_U=b_{\\overline K}^*F\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are \\(x\\in X(k)\\) and \\(m\\ge0\\) such that, for\n\\[\nT=U\\times_S\\mathcal X,\\qquad F_T=\\operatorname{pr}_U^*F_U,\n\\]\n\\[\nQ^a=\n\\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^m u_iv_i+f=0\\right\\}\n\\subset T\\times_S\\mathbb A_S^{2m},\n\\qquad \\tau:Q^a\\to T,\n\\]\none has\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_{Q^a}(\\tau_{\\overline K}^*F_T)\\bigr)_\\nu=0,\n\\qquad\n\\nu=(u,x,0,0).\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\nThe coordinate symbols \\(u_i,v_i\\) are distinct from the point \\(u\\). For \\(m=0\\), this is vanishing on \\(V(f)\\times_S\\mathcal X\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose \\(h,x,m,\\theta=(A',\\xi)\\) from Proposition 3, so\n\\[\np^*A=q^*\\theta\\text{ at }h,\\qquad \\xi\\ne0.\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\nUse the exact-position correspondence \\(H\\), proper bound \\(\\overline H\\), and generic Satake integrand \\(\\mathcal I_{\\overline K}\\) of Lemma 3.1. On the open orbit it is \\(p^*F\\) times an invertible constant complex; suppress that factor after restriction.</p><p>**Proper fiber with zero cohomology.** Put \\(D=V(f)\\), take two copies \\(H_1,H_2\\), and form\n\\[\nW_0=H_2\\times_{p_2,\\mathcal B,b}U,\\qquad\nW=H_2\\times_{\\mathcal B}D.\n\\]\nBoth map to \\(\\mathcal Z=\\mathcal B\\times_S\\mathcal X\\) through \\(q_2\\). The map \\(W_0\\to\\mathcal Z\\) is smooth. At \\(w=(h,u)\\), the equation cutting out \\(W\\) has differential \\(q_2^*\\theta\\). Its relative differential over the output bundle is nonzero because \\(\\xi\\ne0\\). Thus \\(W\\to\\mathcal B\\), by output, is smooth near \\(w\\). Proposition 1 gives\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_W(q_{2,\\overline K}^*\\mathrm H_V(F))\\bigr)_w=0.\n\\tag{5.3}\n\\]</p><p>Form\n\\[\nP=H_1\\times_{\\mathcal Z}W_0,\\qquad\nP_D=H_1\\times_{\\mathcal Z}W\n\\subset\n\\overline P_D=\\overline H_1\\times_{\\mathcal Z}W.\n\\]\nLet \\(\\pi:\\overline P_D\\to W\\) be proper. Let \\(c:P\\to U\\) record the second input and \\(a:P\\to\\mathcal B\\) the first input. The distinguished point is\n\\[\nz=(h,h,u)\\in P_D(k).\n\\]\nThese are algebraic spaces by representability of the fixed-side Hecke maps.</p><p>For the pulled-back Satake integrand \\(\\overline{\\mathcal I}_{\\overline K}\\), generic proper base change identifies\n\\[\nR\\pi_{\\overline K,*}\\overline{\\mathcal I}_{\\overline K}\n\\simeq q_{2,\\overline K}^*\\mathrm H_V(F).\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\nZ_w=\\pi^{-1}(w),\\qquad\n\\mathcal K=\n\\left.\\Psi_{\\overline P_D}\n\\overline{\\mathcal I}_{\\overline K}\\right|_{Z_w}.\n\\]\nProper compatibility and (5.3) give\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(Z_w,\\mathcal K)=0.\n\\tag{5.4}\n\\]</p><p>**Isolate the distinguished stalk.** The map \\(a:P\\to\\mathcal B\\) is smooth: \\(P\\to H_1\\) is a base change of \\(W_0\\to\\mathcal Z\\), and \\(p_1\\) is smooth. At \\((h_1,w)\\) in the exact-position part of \\(Z_w\\), \\(d(f(c))\\) is the pullback of \\(q_1^*\\theta\\). If its relative differential over \\(\\mathcal B\\) is zero, smooth cotangent injectivity for \\(P\\to H_1\\) implies that \\(q_1^*\\theta\\) comes from the input cotangent. Passing to the relative cotangent for \\(\\widetilde p_1=(p_1,\\ell_{H_1})\\) removes the leg term. A necessary condition is\n\\[\nS_{A'}(h_1)=0.\n\\]\nBy Proposition 3 this has an isolated zero at \\(h\\) in the fixed-output-and-leg fiber. Consequently \\(P_D\\to\\mathcal B\\) is smooth at every point of a punctured neighborhood of \\(z\\) in \\(Z_w\\). On the open orbit, the generic integrand is \\(a_{\\overline K}^*F\\). Proposition 1 supplies an open neighborhood \\(O\\subset Z_w\\) with\n\\[\n\\mathcal K|_{O\\setminus\\{z\\}}=0.\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(C_w=Z_w\\setminus O\\), reduced. The disjoint closed subsets \\(\\{z\\},C_w\\) support \\(\\mathcal K\\). Open–closed localization gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal K\\simeq\ni_{z,*}\\mathcal K_z\n\\oplus i_{C_w,*}i_{C_w}^*\\mathcal K,\n\\]\nhence\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(Z_w,\\mathcal K)\n\\simeq\n\\mathcal K_z\\oplus\nR\\Gamma(C_w,i_{C_w}^*\\mathcal K).\n\\]\nEquation (5.4) forces \\(\\mathcal K_z=0\\). Removing the invertible normalization,\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_{P_D}(a_{\\overline K}^*F)\\bigr)_z=0.\n\\tag{5.5}\n\\]\nNo boundary Satake-kernel formula is used.</p><p>**Lift the chart along the whole diagonal.** The diagonal\n\\(\\Delta\\simeq W_0\\subset P\\) repeats the modification. It is a section of the separated smooth map \\(P\\to W_0\\), hence a closed regular immersion near \\(z\\). On it there is a specified isomorphism \\(bc\\simeq a\\).</p><p>After an étale replacement near \\(z\\), construct \\(r:P\\to U\\) and an isomorphism\n\\[\nbr\\simeq a,\\qquad r|_\\Delta=c|_\\Delta,\n\\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nrestricting to the prescribed diagonal isomorphism. Indeed,\n\\[\nE_P=P\\times_{a,\\mathcal B,b}U\\to P\n\\]\nis smooth with a section over \\(\\Delta\\). Take an étale scheme neighborhood in \\(P\\). Since \\(\\mathcal B\\) has affine diagonal, \\(E_P\\to P\\times_SU\\) is affine, so \\(E_P\\) is a scheme. Smooth coordinates give an étale map \\(E_P\\to\\mathbb A_P^e\\) near the section point [24, Tag 039P]. Shrink so that the diagonal section lands there, extend its coordinate functions from the closed diagonal, and pull back the étale map along the extended tuple. Retain the open branch over \\(\\Delta\\) selected by the original section, removing its closed complement in the inverse image of \\(\\Delta\\). This proves (5.6).</p><p>**Exact quadratic coordinates.** Shrink \\(U\\) so that its relative dimension is \\(N\\). The map\n\\[\n\\Delta\\to T=U\\times_S\\mathcal X,\\qquad(c,\\mathrm{leg}),\n\\]\nis smooth of relative dimension \\(m\\). Thus the relative dimensions of \\(T,\\Delta,P\\) are \\(N+1,N+1+m,N+1+2m\\).</p><p>Choose generators \\(v_1,\\ldots,v_m\\) of the diagonal ideal forming a conormal frame. Because \\(r=c\\) on \\(\\Delta\\), there are regular \\(u_1,\\ldots,u_m\\) with the exact identity\n\\[\nf(c)-f(r)=\\sum_{i=1}^m u_iv_i.\n\\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nOn the whole diagonal fiber \\(\\Delta_{(u,x)}\\), the relative differential of \\(f(c)\\) for \\(P\\to W_0\\) is zero, while that of \\(f(r)\\) is induced by \\(d(f_s)_u=b^*A\\). The isomorphism (5.6) is canonical along that whole fiber. Hence\n\\[\n\\sum_i(u_i|_\\Delta)[dv_i]=-S_A\n\\quad\\text{on }\\Delta_{(u,x)}.\n\\tag{5.8}\n\\]\nThus \\(u_i(z)=0\\), and the derivative of \\((u_i|_\\Delta)_i\\) is an isomorphism on \\(T_z\\Delta_{(u,x)}\\), by Proposition 3. Equality only at \\(z\\) would not suffice for this derivative.</p><p>Define\n\\[\n\\Phi=(r,\\mathrm{leg},u_1,\\ldots,u_m,v_1,\\ldots,v_m):\nP\\to T\\times_S\\mathbb A_S^{2m}.\n\\]\nA tangent vector in the kernel is killed first by all \\(dv_i\\), so lies in \\(T_z\\Delta\\); then by \\(dr,d\\mathrm{leg}\\), so lies in \\(T_z\\Delta_{(u,x)}\\); then by the derivative isomorphism in (5.8), so is zero. Equal relative dimensions make \\(d\\Phi_z\\) an isomorphism. The relative Jacobian criterion makes \\(\\Phi\\) étale at \\(z\\).</p><p>Equation (5.7) identifies \\(P_D\\) with the inverse image of \\(Q^a\\), and (5.6) identifies the actual sheaf:\n\\[\na_{\\overline K}^*F\n\\simeq r_{\\overline K}^*F_U\n\\simeq\n\\Phi_{D,\\overline K}^*\\tau_{\\overline K}^*F_T.\n\\]\nÉtale base change turns (5.5) into (5.1).</p><p>For \\(m=0\\), the diagonal is open near \\(z\\), the sum is empty, and the model is \\(D\\times_S\\mathcal X\\). Smooth base change also yields\n\\((\\Psi_D(F_U|_{D_{\\overline K}}))_u=0\\).\nThe argument uses no division by \\(2\\) and no mixed-characteristic singular-support assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "Proposition 6.3 (Hypersurface test)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Work over the trait of Proposition 1 in either characteristic regime.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(F\\) be the object of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(b:U\\to\\mathcal B\\) be smooth, with \\(U\\) a finite-type \\(S\\)-scheme, and put \\(F_U=b_{\\overline K}^*F\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal N_U\\subset T^*U_s\\) be the image of the global nilpotent cone under smooth cotangent pullback.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\in\\Gamma(U,\\mathcal O_U)\\), \\(D=V(f)\\), and \\(u\\in D_s(k)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume \\(d(f_s)_u\\notin(\\mathcal N_U)_u\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_D(F_U|_{D_{\\overline K}})\\bigr)_u=0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>If \\(d(f_s)_u\\) is not in the image of \\(T^*_{b(u)}\\mathcal B_s\\), its relative cotangent image for \\(b\\) is nonzero. The smooth hypersurface criterion makes \\(D\\to\\mathcal B\\) smooth near \\(u\\), and Proposition 1 proves the assertion.</p><p>Otherwise \\(d(f_s)_u=b^*A\\) for non-nilpotent \\(A\\). Proposition 4 supplies \\(x,m\\). Put\n\\[\nT=U\\times_S\\mathcal X,\\quad t_0=(u,x),\\quad\nD_T=D\\times_S\\mathcal X,\n\\]\nand let \\(F_T\\) be the pullback of \\(F_U\\). If \\(m=0\\), Proposition 4 gives vanishing on \\(D_T\\), and smooth compatibility for \\(D_T\\to D\\) finishes.</p><p>For \\(m\\ge1\\), compactify the affine quadratic model to\n\\[\ng:Q_f=\n\\left\\{\\sum_{a=1}^m u_av_a+fw^2=0\\right\\}\n\\subset\\mathbb P_T^{2m}\\to T.\n\\]\nThe fiber over \\(t_0\\) is the split cone. Away from its vertex, some \\(u_a\\) or \\(v_a\\) is nonzero. In the chart \\(u_a=1\\), the equation solves uniquely for \\(v_a\\), so \\(g\\) is smooth there, also in characteristic two. Proposition 1 gives \\(\\Psi_TF_T=0\\); smooth compatibility gives vanishing on all nonvertex points. Proposition 4 gives vanishing at the vertex in the chart \\(w=1\\).</p><p>Thus the nearby-cycle complex of \\(g_{\\overline K}^*F_T\\) vanishes on the whole proper fiber. Proper compatibility and projection formula give\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_T(F_T\\otimes Rg_{\\overline K,*}E)\\bigr)_{t_0}=0.\n\\tag{6.3}\n\\]</p><p>Apply Lemma 5.1 on \\(T_{\\overline K}\\) and tensor with \\(F_T\\). The third term is\n\\[\ni_{\\overline K,*}\n(F_T|_{(D_T)_{\\overline K}})(-m)[-2m].\n\\]\nAfter specialization and taking the stalk at \\(t_0\\), the first term vanishes because \\(\\Psi_TF_T=0\\), the second by (6.3), and therefore the third vanishes. Proper compatibility for \\(i:D_T\\hookrightarrow T\\) identifies it with\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_{D_T}(F_T|_{(D_T)_{\\overline K}})\\bigr)_{t_0}(-m)[-2m].\n\\]\nSmooth compatibility for \\(D_T\\to D\\) proves the result. No purity theorem for the possibly singular hypersurface is used.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 5.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.1 (Quadric comparison), with Remark 6.2", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>This lemma uses a field setting independent of the trait:</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(K_0\\) be algebraically closed of characteristic different from \\(\\ell\\), \\(E=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\), and \\(B\\) a finite-type \\(K_0\\)-scheme.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f\\in\\Gamma(B,\\mathcal O_B)\\), \\(i:D=V(f)\\hookrightarrow B\\), and \\(m\\ge1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  g:Q_f=\n  \\left\\{\\sum_{a=1}^m u_av_a+fw^2=0\\right\\}\n  \\subset\\mathbb P_B^{2m}\\to B.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Hyperplane powers define a distinguished triangle\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{2m-1}E_B(-j)[-2j]\n\\longrightarrow Rg_*E_{Q_f}\n\\longrightarrow i_*E_D(-m)[-2m]\n\\longrightarrow\n\\left(\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{2m-1}E_B(-j)[-2j]\\right)[1].\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\nIdentifying the third term with the displayed Tate line requires only a generator of a fixed one-dimensional vector space. Neither \\(B\\) nor \\(D\\) is assumed smooth or reduced. Rational coefficients are essential to the stated hyperplane-power comparison; no exclusion of \\(\\ell=2\\) is imposed.</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(h=c_1(\\mathcal O_{Q_f}(1))\\). Adjunction applied to \\(h^j\\) gives the displayed map. Let \\(\\mathcal K\\) be its cone and \\(j_B:B\\setminus D\\hookrightarrow B\\).</p><p>A split smooth projective quadric of dimension \\(n\\) has no odd cohomology, one \\(E(-r)\\) in degree \\(2r\\) for \\(0\\le r\\le n\\), and an additional middle copy when \\(n\\) is even. Write its equation as\n\\(x_0x_1+q'(x_2,\\ldots)=0\\).\nThe \\(x_0\\ne0\\) locus is \\(\\mathbb A^n\\); its complement is the cone over a split quadric of dimension \\(n-2\\), and removal of the vertex gives a line bundle over that smaller quadric. This gives the affine-cell filtration, starting with a conic \\(\\mathbb P^1\\) and the split zero-dimensional quadric of two points. Localization and\n\\[\nR\\Gamma_c(\\mathbb A^r,E)=E(-r)[-2r]\n\\]\ngive the groups [18, Example 16.3 and Theorem 23.2]. The top hyperplane power on a positive-dimensional smooth quadric has degree \\(2\\), so every hyperplane power is nonzero in \\(E\\).</p><p>Off \\(D\\), fibers are smooth odd-dimensional quadrics of dimension \\(2m-1\\). Proper base change gives \\(j_B^*\\mathcal K=0\\).</p><p>Over \\(D\\), the family and hyperplane bundle are the product with\n\\[\nV_m=\\left\\{\\sum_{a=1}^m u_av_a=0\\right\\}\\subset\\mathbb P_{K_0}^{2m}.\n\\]\nFor its vertex \\(e=[0:\\cdots:0:1]\\), projection identifies \\(V_m\\setminus\\{e\\}\\) with a line bundle over\n\\[\nC_m=\n\\left\\{\\sum_{a=1}^m u_av_a=0\\right\\}\n\\subset\\mathbb P_{K_0}^{2m-1}.\n\\]\nLocalization yields\n\\[\nH^0(V_m,E)=E,\\qquad H^1(V_m,E)=0,\n\\]\n\\[\nH^r(V_m,E)=H^{r-2}(C_m,E)(-1)\\qquad(r\\ge2).\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\nThus \\(V_m\\) has the cohomology of \\(\\mathbb P^{2m-1}\\), with one extra \\(E(-m)\\) in degree \\(2m\\).</p><p>Resolve the vertex by\n\\[\n\\widetilde V_m=\n\\mathbb P_{\\mathrm{lines},C_m}\n(\\mathcal O_{C_m}(-1)\\oplus\\mathcal O_{C_m})\n\\longrightarrow V_m,\n\\qquad \\pi:\\widetilde V_m\\to C_m.\n\\]\nFor the pulled-back hyperplane class \\(\\zeta\\) and \\(h_C=c_1(\\mathcal O_{C_m}(1))\\), the projective-bundle relation is\n\\(\\zeta^2=\\pi^*h_C\\,\\zeta\\).\nTherefore\n\\[\n\\int_{\\widetilde V_m}\\zeta^{2m-1}\n=\n\\int_{C_m}h_C^{2m-2}=2.\n\\]\nThe top hyperplane power on \\(V_m\\), and hence all lower ones, is nonzero. For \\(m=1\\), this is the union of two projective lines meeting at the vertex; the resolution is their disjoint union.</p><p>The cone of\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{2m-1}E(-j)[-2j]\\to R\\Gamma(V_m,E)\n\\]\nhas exactly one cohomology group, \\(E(-m)\\) in degree \\(2m\\), so is \\(E(-m)[-2m]\\). Proper base change makes \\(i^*\\mathcal K\\) the constant pullback of that cone. Open–closed localization,\n\\[\n(j_B)_!j_B^*\\mathcal K\\to\\mathcal K\\to i_*i^*\\mathcal K\\to,\n\\]\nidentifies \\(\\mathcal K=i_*E_D(-m)[-2m]\\). No middle-cohomology local system is left unidentified.</p><p>These compatibilities hold first with finite coefficients and in their adic forms [24, Tags 095L, 0DDE, 09AH, 09C9], then after rationalization and extension to \\(E\\). With \\(\\mathbb Z/2^a\\)-coefficients the comparison need not be an isomorphism off \\(D\\), since a conic’s hyperplane degree is \\(2\\). The lemma uses rational coefficients, including when \\(\\ell=2\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "Proposition 6.5 (Higher-codimension test)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the trait, \\(F\\), smooth chart \\(b:U\\to\\mathcal B\\), sheaf \\(F_U\\), and cone \\(\\mathcal N_U\\) of Proposition 5.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u\\in U_s(k)\\) and let \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_d\\) be regular functions vanishing at \\(u\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Assume their special-fiber differentials are independent and\n  \\[\n  \\operatorname{span}_k\\{d(f_{1,s})_u,\\ldots,d(f_{d,s})_u\\}\n  \\cap(\\mathcal N_U)_u=\\{0\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>For \\(J=V(f_1,\\ldots,f_d)\\),\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_J(F_U|_{J_{\\overline K}})\\bigr)_u=0.\n\\]\nThe case \\(d=0\\), with \\(J=U\\), is included.</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(d=0\\), use Proposition 1. For \\(d\\ge1\\), form\n\\[\nr:I=\n\\left\\{\\sum_{a=1}^dc_af_a=0\\right\\}\n\\subset U\\times_S\\mathbb P_S^{d-1}\\to U.\n\\]\nEach affine projective-space chart is smooth over \\(\\mathcal B\\). On \\(c_a=1\\), the equation is\n\\[\nf_a+\\sum_{b\\ne a}c_bf_b=0.\n\\]\nAt \\((u,[c])\\), its special-fiber differential is\n\\[\n\\sum_{b=1}^dc_b\\,d(f_{b,s})_u.\n\\]\nThe projective-parameter component is zero because \\(f_b(u)=0\\). The displayed combination is nonzero by independence and outside \\((\\mathcal N_U)_u\\) by assumption. The nilpotent cone on the product chart is the pullback cone with zero parameter component.</p><p>Proposition 5 gives nearby-cycle vanishing at every closed point over \\(u\\). The restriction is constructible, and a nonzero constructible cohomology sheaf over \\(k\\) has a nonzero stalk at a closed \\(k\\)-point. Hence it vanishes on the whole projective fiber. Proper compatibility gives\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_U Rr_{\\overline K,*}r_{\\overline K}^*F_U\\bigr)_u=0.\n\\]</p><p>Tensor Lemma 6.1 on \\(U_{\\overline K}\\) with \\(F_U\\). The first term is a finite sum of shifts and twists of \\(F_U\\), the second is the displayed proper pushforward, and the third is\n\\[\ni_{\\overline K,*}(F_U|_{J_{\\overline K}})\n(-(d-1))[-2(d-1)].\n\\]\nThe first two specialized stalks vanish. Proper compatibility for the closed immersion \\(i\\) identifies the third with the corresponding shift and twist of the required stalk. It therefore vanishes as well. For \\(d=1\\), the first sum is empty.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 6.1", "orig": "Lemma 6.4 (Incidence comparison)", "depth": 1, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(B\\) be a finite-type scheme over an algebraically closed field of characteristic different from \\(\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_d\\in\\Gamma(B,\\mathcal O_B)\\), \\(d\\ge1\\), and \\(i:J=V(f_1,\\ldots,f_d)\\hookrightarrow B\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  r:I=\n  \\left\\{\\sum_{a=1}^dc_af_a=0\\right\\}\n  \\subset B\\times\\mathbb P^{d-1}\\to B.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Hyperplane powers give a distinguished triangle\n\\[\n\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{d-2}E_B(-j)[-2j]\n\\longrightarrow Rr_*E_I\n\\longrightarrow i_*E_J(-(d-1))[-2(d-1)]\n\\longrightarrow\n\\left(\\bigoplus_{j=0}^{d-2}E_B(-j)[-2j]\\right)[1].\n\\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nFor \\(d=1\\), the first term is zero and \\(I=J\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(d=1\\), the equation in \\(B\\times\\mathbb P^0\\) is \\(f_1=0\\).</p><p>For \\(d\\ge2\\), off \\(J\\) the map\n\\(\\mathcal O_B^d\\to\\mathcal O_B\\) with coefficients \\(f_a\\) is surjective. Its kernel has rank \\(d-1\\), and \\(I\\) is its projective bundle of lines. Restricted hyperplane powers identify its direct image with the first term; equivalently, use proper base change and the cohomology of \\(\\mathbb P^{d-2}\\).</p><p>Over \\(J\\), the family is \\(J\\times\\mathbb P^{d-1}\\), with hyperplane class from the second factor. The restricted cone is its constant top class\n\\[\nE_J(-(d-1))[-2(d-1)].\n\\]\nThe global cone vanishes off \\(J\\). Open–closed localization identifies it with the pushforward of this restricted cone.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "Lemma 7.1 (Extending a generic bundle)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal X\\to\\operatorname{Spec}R\\) be the smooth proper curve of Proposition 1.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(G/R\\) be its split connected reductive group.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\overline K\\), an algebraic closure of \\(\\operatorname{Frac}R\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Every \\(G\\)-bundle on \\(\\mathcal X_{\\overline K}\\) extends to a \\(G\\)-bundle on \\(\\mathcal X_{R'}\\) for some finite extension \\(\\operatorname{Frac}(R')/\\operatorname{Frac}(R)\\) inside \\(\\overline K\\), where \\(R'\\) is the integral closure of \\(R\\) in that extension.</p>", "proof": "<p>By [8, Theorem 2 and Remark 2(b)], a \\(G\\)-bundle \\(P_{\\overline K}\\) is trivial on a nonempty open subset of \\(\\mathcal X_{\\overline K}\\). The result applies to connected reductive groups over the algebraically closed field \\(\\overline K\\).</p><p>Choose a trivialization away from distinct points \\(x_1,\\ldots,x_r\\). Gluing at them gives a chain\n\\[\nP_0\\dashrightarrow P_1\\dashrightarrow\\cdots\n\\dashrightarrow P_r=P_{\\overline K},\n\\qquad P_0\\text{ trivial},\n\\]\nwith \\(i\\)-th leg \\(x_i\\). Choose a Schubert bound for each relative position.</p><p>Starting with the trivial bundle over \\(S\\), form the space of chains satisfying these bounds with variable legs. At every stage the bounded Hecke space is representable and proper over the preceding bundle and new leg, by Lemma 3.1. Since \\(\\mathcal X/S\\) is proper, the chain space is a proper finite-type algebraic space over \\(S\\), carrying the universal final bundle.</p><p>The chosen chain is a \\(\\overline K\\)-point and descends to a finite extension of \\(\\operatorname{Frac}R\\), because the space is finite type and \\(\\overline K\\) is algebraic over that field. The valuative criterion extends it over \\(R'\\). Pull back the universal final bundle. Successive one-point modifications allow the legs to coincide on the closed fiber.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "Lemma 7.2 (A slice with nonzero nearby cycles)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p>This proposition uses the following scheme-theoretic setting:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(R\\) is a complete discrete valuation ring with algebraically closed residue field \\(k\\) of characteristic different from \\(\\ell\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(S=\\operatorname{Spec}R\\), and \\(\\overline K\\) is an algebraic closure of its fraction field.</p><p class=\"li\">\\(U/S\\) is smooth affine of constant relative dimension \\(N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\mathcal F\\) is a bounded constructible \\(E\\)-complex on \\(U_{\\overline K}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">\\(\\Lambda\\subset T^*U_s\\) is closed conical with \\(\\dim\\Lambda\\le N\\).</p><p class=\"li\">The closure in \\(U\\) of the nonzero-stalk locus of \\(\\mathcal F\\) meets \\(U_s\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>After a finite trait extension and shrinking \\(U\\), there are \\(u\\in U_s(k)\\), \\(0\\le d\\le N\\), and regular functions \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_d\\) vanishing at \\(u\\), with independent special-fiber differentials, such that\n\\[\n\\operatorname{span}\\{d(f_{1,s})_u,\\ldots,d(f_{d,s})_u\\}\n\\cap\\Lambda_u\\subseteq\\{0\\},\n\\]\nand, for \\(J=V(f_1,\\ldots,f_d)\\),\n\\[\n\\bigl(\\Psi_J(\\mathcal F|_{J_{\\overline K}})\\bigr)_u\\ne0.\n\\tag{7.1}\n\\]\nFor \\(d=0\\), the list is empty and \\(J=U\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>**Descend finite support data.** Let \\(A_1,\\ldots,A_t\\) be the irreducible components of the closure of the nonzero-stalk locus in \\(U_{\\overline K}\\). Constructibility supplies proper closed \\(B_i\\subsetneq A_i\\) such that every stalk on \\(A_i\\setminus B_i\\) is nonzero. Give these sets reduced structures. Their finite ideal data and containments descend to a common finite extension of the fraction field. Replace the trait. This does not assert that \\(\\mathcal F\\) descends.</p><p>The new support closure still meets the special fiber: the finite map of models maps the new closure onto the old one, since it is closed and agrees on geometric generic support.</p><p>Let \\(\\overline A_i,\\overline B_i\\) be horizontal closures and \\(e_i=\\dim A_i\\). Each \\(\\overline A_i\\) is integral and dominates \\(S\\). Every nonempty special fiber is pure of dimension \\(e_i\\) [24, Tag 0B2J]. Applying this to components of \\(B_i\\) gives\n\\[\n\\dim(\\overline B_i)_s&lt;e_i\n\\]\nwhenever nonempty. Completeness makes the trait excellent and universally catenary [24, Tag 07QW]. The dimension formula at a closed \\(v\\in(\\overline A_i)_s\\) gives\n\\[\n\\dim\\mathcal O_{\\overline A_i,v}=e_i+1.\n\\tag{7.2}\n\\]</p><p>**Choose the special point.** Let \\(d\\) be maximal among \\(e_i\\) with nonempty special fiber. Choose a reduced irreducible special-fiber component \\(T\\) of dimension \\(d\\). At a general closed point of \\(T\\), its reduced structure is smooth, no other distinct reduced component of \\(\\bigcup_i(\\overline A_i)_s\\) passes through the point, and none of the \\(\\overline B_i\\) passes through it. Bad special loci have dimension below \\(d\\). Different horizontal components may have the same special component \\(T\\); retain all of them.</p><p>One may also arrange\n\\[\n\\dim\\Lambda_u\\le N-d.\n\\tag{7.3}\n\\]\nFor the restriction of \\(\\Lambda\\) to \\(T\\), exclude components not dominating \\(T\\). Every remaining generic fiber has dimension at most \\(N-d\\), since \\(\\dim\\Lambda\\le N\\), and the same bound holds on a nonempty open. Choose \\(u\\in T(k)\\) satisfying all conditions.</p><p>**Choose transverse equations.** Let \\(V=T_u^*U_s\\), of dimension \\(N\\). For \\(d&gt;0\\), a general \\(d\\)-plane \\(L\\subset V\\) avoids \\(\\Lambda_u\\setminus\\{0\\}\\): by (7.3),\n\\[\n\\dim\\mathbb P(\\Lambda_u)\\le N-d-1,\n\\]\nso the incidence of planes meeting it is proper closed in \\(\\operatorname{Gr}(d,V)\\). The condition\n\\[\nL\\to T_u^*T\n\\]\nbe an isomorphism is another nonempty open. The Grassmannian is irreducible, so the opens meet. For \\(d=0\\), take \\(L=0\\).</p><p>Lift a basis of \\(L\\) to regular functions \\(f_a\\) vanishing at \\(u\\). Their restrictions to smooth \\(T\\) form a regular system of parameters. Thus, for\n\\[\nJ=V(f_1,\\ldots,f_d),\\qquad\nZ=J\\cap\\bigcup_i\\overline A_i,\n\\]\nthe point \\(u\\) is isolated in \\(Z_s\\), after shrinking. This concerns the whole union: its reduced special fiber near \\(u\\) is \\(T\\), even when several horizontal components specialize to \\(T\\).</p><p>**Find a generic generization.** Choose \\(i\\) with \\(e_i=d\\) and \\(T\\) a component of \\((\\overline A_i)_s\\). By (7.2), \\(\\mathcal O_{\\overline A_i,u}\\) has dimension \\(d+1\\). Cutting by \\(d\\) functions leaves dimension at least one; quotienting also by a uniformizer \\(\\pi\\) leaves dimension zero. Some prime of the cut local ring avoids \\(\\pi\\), since otherwise \\(\\pi\\) would be nilpotent and the ring would have dimension zero. This yields a generic-fiber point \\(z\\) specializing to \\(u\\).</p><p>The map \\(Z\\to S\\) is quasi-finite at \\(u\\), since \\(u\\) is an isolated closed fiber point [24, Tag 01TH]. Its open quasi-finite locus contains \\(z\\), so the residue field of \\(z\\) is finite over \\(\\operatorname{Frac}R\\). Its geometric lifts avoid \\(B_i\\): a point of the closed \\(\\overline B_i\\) could not specialize to \\(u\\notin\\overline B_i\\). Each lift has nonzero stalk.</p><p>**Compute on a finite neighborhood.** Take an affine neighborhood of \\(u\\) in the quasi-finite locus. Henselian decomposition [24, Tag 04GJ] gives an open neighborhood \\(Q\\subset Z\\), finite over \\(S\\), whose special fiber is only \\(u\\). Equivalently, embed the separated quasi-finite neighborhood in a finite \\(S\\)-scheme by Zariski’s Main Theorem and take the henselian local factor at \\(u\\); an open subset of a local spectrum containing its closed point is the whole spectrum. Every generization of \\(u\\), including \\(z\\), lies in \\(Q\\).</p><p>Remove \\(Z\\setminus Q\\) from \\(J\\) by removing that closed subset from the ambient \\(U\\), then take an affine neighborhood of \\(u\\). It contains all of \\(Q\\), because every point of \\(Q\\) specializes to \\(u\\). The new \\(J\\) is cut out by the same functions in a smooth affine chart, its nearby-cycle stalk is unchanged, and \\(Q\\) is closed in \\(J\\).</p><p>For \\(\\iota:Q\\hookrightarrow J\\) and\n\\(\\mathcal H=\\mathcal F|_{Q_{\\overline K}}\\), localization gives\n\\[\n\\mathcal F|_{J_{\\overline K}}\n\\simeq\\iota_{\\overline K,*}\\mathcal H.\n\\]\nProper compatibility reduces (7.1) to \\((\\Psi_Q\\mathcal H)_u\\). Since \\(Q/S\\) is finite with special fiber only \\(u\\),\n\\[\n(\\Psi_Q\\mathcal H)_u\n\\simeq\n\\Psi_S R\\Gamma(Q_{\\overline K},\\mathcal H)\n\\simeq R\\Gamma(Q_{\\overline K},\\mathcal H).\n\\]\nThe finite \\(\\overline K\\)-scheme has the étale topos of a finite discrete set, so\n\\[\nR\\Gamma(Q_{\\overline K},\\mathcal H)\n\\simeq\n\\bigoplus_{x\\in|Q_{\\overline K}|}\\mathcal H_x.\n\\]\nAt least one summand is nonzero. This calculation uses geometric nearby cycles with monodromy forgotten, not inertia invariants. It is compatible with finite coefficients, the \\(\\ell\\)-adic limit, inversion of \\(\\ell\\), and extension to \\(E\\), without a field of descent for \\(\\mathcal F\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "Lemma 8.3 (A continuous coherent right adjoint)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Let \\(A_0\\) be a compactly generated presentable stable \\(E\\)-linear symmetric monoidal category.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume its compact objects are dualizable and its tensor product preserves colimits separately.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(a:A_0\\to\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\) be continuous, \\(E\\)-linear, and symmetric monoidal.</p><p class=\"li\">Give \\(\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\) its \\(A_0\\)-action through \\(a\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The right adjoint \\(R_a\\) of \\(a\\) is continuous and has a coherent \\(A_0\\)-linear structure. Moreover,\n\\[\nR_a(E)\\ne0.\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Presentability gives the right adjoint. A compact object is dualizable, so its image under \\(a\\) is a perfect \\(E\\)-complex. Thus \\(a\\) preserves compact objects. Testing against compact generators and using adjunction shows that \\(R_a\\) preserves filtered colimits. It is exact because the categories are stable; exactness and filtered-colimit preservation imply all-colimit preservation.</p><p>The canonical projection morphism is\n\\[\nb\\otimes R_a(V)\\longrightarrow R_a(a(b)\\otimes V).\n\\]\nFor dualizable \\(b\\), test maps from arbitrary \\(c\\):\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{RHom}_{A_0}(c,b\\otimes R_a(V))\n&amp;\\simeq\n\\operatorname{RHom}_{A_0}(b^\\vee\\otimes c,R_a(V))\\\\\n&amp;\\simeq\n\\operatorname{RHom}_E(a(b)^\\vee\\otimes a(c),V)\\\\\n&amp;\\simeq\n\\operatorname{RHom}_E(a(c),a(b)\\otimes V).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nHence the projection morphism is invertible for dualizable \\(b\\). Both sides preserve colimits in \\(b\\); compact generation gives invertibility for all \\(b\\).</p><p>These morphisms are adjunction mates of the module structure on \\(a\\), so unit and associativity compatibilities hold before imposing invertibility. They therefore define a coherent module-functor structure. Finally,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{RHom}_{A_0}(\\mathbf1,R_a(E))\n\\simeq\\operatorname{RHom}_E(E,E)\\simeq E,\n\\]\nso \\(R_a(E)\\ne0\\). Compactness of the unit is not needed.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "Lemma 9.1 (Coefficient comparison, including unbounded objects)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Put \\(E_0=\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\), \\(E=\\overline{\\mathbb Q}_\\ell\\), and \\(k=\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(X/k\\) be smooth projective connected and \\(G/k\\) connected reductive satisfying Hypothesis 1.1.</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite-type \\(k\\)-scheme \\(T\\), \\(\\operatorname{Shv}_L(T)\\), \\(L=E_0,E\\), is the ind-completion of bounded constructible \\(L\\)-adic complexes.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(T\\) is smooth and \\(N\\subset T^*T\\) is closed conical, let \\(P_{N,L}(T)\\) be constructible perverse sheaves with singular support in \\(N\\). The category \\(\\operatorname{Shv}_{N,L}(T)\\) consists of objects whose perverse cohomology belongs to \\(\\operatorname{Ind}(P_{N,L}(T))\\) in every degree.</p><p class=\"li\">This cohomological category need not equal the ind-completion of support-bounded constructible complexes.</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_0=\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp},E_0}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G),\n  \\qquad\n  \\mathcal C_E=\\operatorname{Shv}_{\\mathrm{Nilp},E}(\\operatorname{Bun}_G).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>Coefficient extension induces equivalences\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\otimes_{E_0}\\operatorname{Shv}_{E_0}(T)\n\\simeq\\operatorname{Shv}_E(T),\n\\tag{9.1}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\otimes_{E_0}\\operatorname{QLisse}_{E_0}(X)\n\\simeq\\operatorname{QLisse}_E(X),\n\\tag{9.2}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\otimes_{E_0}\\mathcal C_0\n\\simeq\\mathcal C_E.\n\\tag{9.3}\n\\]\nThe first holds on locally finite-type algebraic stacks by smooth descent. The second is symmetric monoidal and respects the tensor-functor \\(t\\)-structures. The third respects the cohomological nilpotent condition on smooth charts. Free and forgetful functors are \\(t\\)-exact for the ordinary and perverse \\(t\\)-structures where used.</p>", "proof": "<p>**Ambient constructible comparison.** For a finite extension \\(L/E_0\\), extension and restriction preserve bounded constructibles. Mapping complexes between extended objects are obtained by tensoring with \\(L\\). Every \\(L\\)-constructible \\(M\\) is a retract of\n\\[\nL\\otimes_{E_0}\\operatorname{Res}_{L/E_0}M,\n\\]\nusing the separability idempotent. Thus induced compact objects have the required mappings and generate after retracts.</p><p>Constructible \\(E\\)-complexes and their morphisms are obtained through finite coefficient fields [17, Proposition 5.2, Theorem 7.7, Lemma 7.9, Proposition 8.2, Corollary 8.3; 16, §2]. The cohomological finiteness condition [17, (8.1)] holds on finite-type charts over \\(k\\) by [17, Lemma 8.6(1)]. Ind-completion proves (9.1).</p><p>**Detect the cohomological support condition.** Regard the left side of (9.1) as internal \\(E\\)-modules in \\(\\operatorname{Shv}_{E_0}(T)\\), with free and forgetful functors \\(F,U\\). They are ordinary- and perverse-\\(t\\)-exact. For a constructible \\(E\\)-object with finite-field form \\(M_L\\),\n\\[\nU(M_L\\otimes_LE)\n=\n\\underset{\\substack{L\\subset L'\\subset E\\\\{}[L':E_0]&lt;\\infty}}\n{\\operatorname{colim}}\n\\operatorname{Res}_{L'/E_0}(M_L\\otimes_LL').\n\\tag{9.4}\n\\]\nFinite extension and restriction are \\(t\\)-exact, and ind-\\(t\\)-structures commute with filtered colimits.</p><p>On bounded constructibles, coefficient extension preserves and reflects singular-support bounds. The defining test pairs express the bound by universal local acyclicity. Use its dualizability criterion [16, Theorem 1.6]. Composition in the correspondence category uses pullback, tensor, and \\(f_!\\), all compatible with extension by [16, §2]; extending duality data preserves universal local acyclicity.</p><p>For reflection, if a bounded constructible complex over finite \\(L/E_0\\) becomes universally locally acyclic over \\(E\\), its relative dual is constructible [16, Proposition 3.4(ii)]. The dual, evaluation, coevaluation, and triangle identities are finite data on the fixed test schemes and products; [17, Lemma 7.9] descends them to a common finite coefficient extension. Finite extension reflects local acyclicity, because forgetting it makes the test a nonzero finite direct sum of the original test. Finite restriction preserves the same bound: after a splitting extension it is a finite direct sum of coefficient conjugates. The same arguments, or finite-rank local-system descriptions, apply to lissity.</p><p>Consequently \\(F,U\\) preserve the required ind-perverse hearts; for \\(U\\), use (9.4). Finite rank over \\(E\\) is not treated as finite rank over \\(E_0\\).</p><p>Conversely, let \\(P\\) be a perverse internal \\(E\\)-module with \\(U(P)\\) in the required \\(E_0\\)-ind-heart. The counit\n\\[\nF(U(P))\\to P\n\\]\nis an epimorphism in the module heart: its underlying map splits by \\(u\\mapsto1\\otimes u\\). Its source lies in the required \\(E\\)-ind-heart, which is closed under quotients by the Serre property. Thus \\(P\\) lies there. Degree by degree, an \\(E\\)-module satisfies the support condition exactly when its underlying \\(E_0\\)-object does.</p><p>For \\(\\operatorname{QLisse}\\), use the zero-cone ind-perverse heart and its equivalence with the ordinary-cohomological definition [3, §1.2.9]. No claim is made that ordinary lisse sheaves form a Serre subcategory of the full ordinary heart.</p><p>**Scalar extension and descent.** The perverse Serre condition gives finite-cone closure. Perverse cohomology commutes with filtered colimits; these subcategories are closed under filtered colimits, arbitrary sums, hence all colimits and bar realizations. They are closed under scalar tensors. Internal modules in them are exactly ambient modules with underlying object in them. Free-forgetful bar resolutions identify these module categories with categorical scalar extensions. This proves (9.2) and chartwise singular-support comparison, including unbounded objects.</p><p>Ordinary tensor and structural maps commute with extension, giving the monoidal assertion. Connective free modules generate connective modules under connective colimits by the same bar resolution, so tensor \\(t\\)-structures agree.</p><p>Take smooth-chart limits for stacks. Internal modules commute with these limits. Equivalently, \\(\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\), as the module category of the continuous monad \\(E\\otimes_{E_0}(-)\\), is dualizable as a presentable \\(E_0\\)-linear category [10, Lemma 1.6.3]; tensoring with it preserves limits. Chartwise support comparison gives (9.3).</p><p>No scalar extension is exchanged with an unspecified left completion. The argument applies also to \\(\\operatorname{QLisse}(\\mathbb P^1)\\), without identifying it with \\(\\operatorname{IndLisse}(\\mathbb P^1)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "Lemma 9.2 (Spectral coefficient comparison)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(E_0,E,k,X,G,\\check G_0,\\check G,Y_0,Y\\) of Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Both prestacks use right-\\(t\\)-exact symmetric monoidal tensor functors into the relevant \\(\\operatorname{QLisse}\\)-valued affine families.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>There are natural identifications\n\\[\nY_0\\times_{\\operatorname{Spec}E_0}\\operatorname{Spec}E\\simeq Y,\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\otimes_{E_0}\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y_0)\n\\simeq\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y).\n\\tag{9.5}\n\\]\nThe second is symmetric monoidal. They compare universal evaluation for every finite set of legs, including tensor products and collisions.</p>", "proof": "<p>For a connective commutative \\(E\\)-algebra \\(A\\), Proposition 10 and associativity give\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Mod}_A\\otimes_{E_0}\\operatorname{QLisse}_{E_0}(X)\n\\simeq\n\\operatorname{Mod}_A\\otimes_E\\operatorname{QLisse}_E(X)\n\\]\nsymmetrically monoidally. Tensor \\(t\\)-structures are generated by connective factors, equivalently module \\(t\\)-structures with \\(t\\)-exact forgetful functor [3, §1.4.1]; Proposition 10 identifies them.</p><p>For the split dual group,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\otimes_{E_0}\\operatorname{Rep}_{E_0}(\\check G_0)\n\\simeq\\operatorname{Rep}_E(\\check G)\n\\]\nas symmetric monoidal categories with \\(t\\)-structure. Finite-dimensional representations in the heart generate connective objects. Split highest-weight irreducibles are defined over \\(E_0\\) and remain irreducible after extension [19, Theorem 22.2, §§22.3–22.5, Theorem 22.42].</p><p>The universal property of scalar extension identifies the tensor functors into the displayed target, and right-\\(t\\)-exactness agrees. This proves the prestack assertion on every connective affine \\(E\\)-algebra, using the moduli definition [3, §1.4.2], not just field-valued parameters.</p><p>Present \\(Y_0\\) as the colimit of its affine tests. Base change gives a presentation by their base changes, and quasi-coherent categories form the associated limit. On an affine,\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\otimes_{E_0}\\operatorname{Mod}_B\n\\simeq\\operatorname{Mod}_{B\\otimes_{E_0}E}.\n\\]\nThe affine pullbacks are continuous. Dualizability of \\(\\operatorname{Vect}_E\\), as in Proposition 10, permits passage through the limit, proving the second equivalence and its monoidal structure. No finite-dimensionality of \\(E/E_0\\) is required.</p><p>Evaluation of a representation at the parametrized tensor functor is unchanged. Naturality in representations, affine tests, and maps of finite sets identifies the full evaluation system.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "Lemma 9.3 (Comparison of spectral actions)", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain the rational geometric setting of Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Add \\(X\\ne\\mathbb P^1\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Equip \\(\\mathcal C_0\\) with the given rational spectral-action datum.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the coefficient identifications of Propositions 10 and 11.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>The scalar extension of the given \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y_0)\\)-action is the established \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\)-action on \\(\\mathcal C_E\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(X\\ne\\mathbb P^1\\),\n\\[\n\\operatorname{QLisse}_E(X)=\\operatorname{IndLisse}_E(X)\n\\]\n[3, §1.3.3 and §E.2.8]. The equality holds over \\(E_0\\) as well. Extend an object over \\(E_0\\), express it using constructible lisse generators over \\(E\\), and forget coefficients. Formula (9.4) puts the forgotten generators in the colimit closure of \\(E_0\\)-constructible lisse objects. The original object is a retract of its extension followed by forgetting, using an \\(E_0\\)-linear retraction \\(E\\to E_0\\) of the unit. Constructible lisse objects are compact in the ambient ind-constructible category. Thus these categories are compactly generated and dualizable, and exterior products and affine-limit evaluations commute with the coefficient comparisons.</p><p>Fix geometric Satake over \\(E_0\\) [20, Theorem 14.1], and its scalar extension over \\(E\\), with the same dual-group identification, shifts, and geometric Tate lines. Compare the full finite-set monoidal diagram, not merely highest-weight objects.</p><p>On bounded Schubert supports, extension commutes with exterior products, pullbacks, descent, and proper convolution pushforwards. It is perverse-\\(t\\)-exact, so commutes with intermediate extension, described as the image of perverse extension by zero to perverse direct image. It carries Satake IC objects, convolution maps, fusion diagrams, units, and symmetries to those over \\(E\\). Satake fiber and weight functors identify the same split dual group [20, §§4–6 and Theorem 14.1].</p><p>The derived functors are the derived extension of naive Satake. The compatible monoidal diagram in shifted perverse hearts is extended using the bounded-derived universal property [10, §§B.3.5–B.3.8]. Its right-lax monoidal structure is strict; restrict to compact representations and ind-extend. Apply this construction to the whole coefficient-comparison diagram. Naturality compares derived functors and finite-set structural maps. The correspondence action [10, §§B.2.5–B.2.10] compares the continuous Hecke systems, units, composition, and base-change maps.</p><p>Factorization through nilpotent sheaves with \\(\\operatorname{QLisse}\\)-valued legs uses fully faithful exterior-product functors [3, §14.2.5]. Proposition 10 compares these subcategories; Proposition 11 compares evaluation. By the rational datum, the extended action restricts to exactly the established coherent Hecke system over \\(E\\). The equivalence of spaces of actions [3, Theorem 8.1.4] identifies it with the established spectral action [3, Theorem 14.3.2].</p><p>No Frobenius structure or normalized rational Langlands functor is chosen here.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "Lemma 9.4 (Detection of a clopen support), with the full-support input made explicit", "depth": 0, "snippet": "Assumptions / Conditions / Definitions.", "statement": "<span class=\"lbl sub\">Assumptions</span><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(E_0,E,k,X,G,Y_0,Y,\\mathcal C_0,\\mathcal C_E\\) of Theorem 2.</p><p class=\"li\">Assume the coefficient comparisons (9.3) and (9.5) identify the rational and geometric spectral actions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use as an explicit input a \\(\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\)-linear equivalence\n  \\[\n  \\mathcal C_E\\simeq\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y).\n  \\]\n  In the paper this is supplied by full support in regime A.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(U\\subset Y_0\\) be open and closed, and assume its structure idempotent acts by zero on \\(\\mathcal C_0\\).</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\(U=\\varnothing\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Let \\(e_U\\) be \\(\\mathcal O_U\\) extended by zero. Its coefficient extension is \\(e_{U_E}\\), for \\(U_E=U\\times_{E_0}E\\). Action compatibility and generation by scalar-extended objects imply that \\(e_{U_E}\\) acts by zero on all of \\(\\mathcal C_E\\).</p><p>Under the supplied full spectral-linear equivalence, apply it to\n\\[\n\\jmath_Y(\\mathcal O_Y),\n\\qquad\n\\jmath_Y:\n\\operatorname{QCoh}(Y)\\xrightarrow{\\Upsilon_Y}\n\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\{0\\}}(Y)\n\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{IndCoh}_{\\mathrm{Nilp}}(Y).\n\\]\nThe value is \\(\\jmath_Y(e_{U_E})\\). Full faithfulness implies \\(e_{U_E}=0\\), hence \\(U_E=\\varnothing\\).</p><p>For any affine test \\(T=\\operatorname{Spec}A\\to Y_0\\), the inverse image of \\(U\\) is an open-and-closed affine summand defined by an idempotent of \\(\\pi_0(A)\\). It becomes empty over \\(A\\otimes_{E_0}E\\). Faithful flatness forces the idempotent to vanish. This holds on every affine test, so \\(U=\\varnothing\\), without a rational-point description of components.</p>"}], "edges": [[0, 1, "cite"], [0, 2, "cite"], [0, 3, "cite"], [0, 4, "cite"], [1, 5, "cite"], [3, 5, "cite"], [6, 0, "cite"], [6, 15, "cite"], [6, 17, "cite"], [6, 18, "cite"], [6, 20, "cite"], [6, 22, "cite"], [7, 6, "cite"], [7, 9, "cite"], [8, 6, "cite"], [8, 9, "cite"], [9, 6, "cite"], [10, 15, "cite"], [11, 10, "cite"], [11, 12, "cite"], [12, 6, "cite"], [12, 10, "cite"], [13, 10, "cite"], [14, 10, "cite"], [15, 17, "cite"], [16, 15, "cite"], [16, 17, "cite"], [16, 22, "cite"], [17, 18, "cite"], [18, 20, "cite"], [19, 18, "cite"], [20, 0, "cite"], [21, 20, "cite"], [22, 0, "cite"], [23, 0, "cite"], [24, 4, "cite"], [25, 1, "cite"], [25, 26, "cite"], [25, 27, "cite"], [26, 1, "cite"], [26, 27, "cite"], [27, 1, "cite"], [28, 1, "cite"]]});
